AB  - The pursuit of sustainable and high-performance materials for wind turbine blade (WTB) applications has become increasingly critical in light of global environmental challenges and the growing demand for renewable energy. This study presents a systematic scientometric and comprehensive review of industrial waste materials, specifically marble dust, granite dust, and stone dust, as potential reinforcements in composite materials for WTBs. Through an extensive analysis of recent literature and scientometric mapping, this review identifies key trends, research hotspots, and knowledge gaps in utilizing these waste materials. The integration of such by-products not only promotes sustainable resource utilization but also enhances the mechanical, thermal, and durability properties of composite materials. Furthermore, the study explores the environmental benefits and cost-effectiveness of incorporating these industrial wastes, addressing critical aspects of circular economy and sustainability. The findings underscore the role of innovative material engineering approaches in advancing the performance of WTBs while simultaneously addressing environmental concerns. This review aims to provide a robust foundation for researchers and industry professionals exploring the use of industrial waste materials in the renewable energy sector.
