UR  - https://doi.org/10.1016/j.jobe.2026.115970
AB  - Glass recycling still faces significant obstacles, as the chain is often marked by low capillarity of selective collection, material contamination, unfavorable logistics (high weight and low value per volume), and an irregular supply of processed cullet, which limits circularity on a large scale. In parallel, the market for agglomerated rocks is expanding, increasing demand for raw materials and opening up space for higher-value-added recycling routes. In this context, this study proposes an alternative, aligned with the circular economy, to valorize post-consumer soda-lime glass from beverage bottles for the production of vitreous agglomerated rocks with an epoxy matrix, with applications in civil construction. The slabs were produced with 89% (w/w) recycled glass and 11% (w/w) epoxy resin, using a coupling agent and vacuum-assisted hot pressing followed by thermal post-curing. A simplex-centroid packing design selected two optimal particle size mixtures: #4 (50% coarse + 50% medium) and #8 (66% coarse + 17% medium + 17% fine). Chemical-mineralogical analysis (XRF/XRD/SEM-EDS) confirmed the typical soda-lime composition (SiO 2 –Na 2 O–CaO) and the predominantly amorphous structure. The slabs exhibited low porosity and low water absorption (P = 0.25–0.36%; E = 0.08–0.14%) and high flexural strength (44.13 ± 1.99 MPa for #4; 42.98 ± 1.08 MPa for #8), surpassing comparative commercial products used in the research, Branco PRIME (36.78 ± 3.98 MPa) and Branco SIENA (13.50 ± 1.10 MPa), an agglomerated rock and an ornamental rock. Thermal analysis by dilatometry indicated greater stability for #4 beyond ∼580 °C. The results demonstrate a technically consistent route to convert post-consumer soda-lime glass into higher-value-added vitreous agglomerated rocks, with performance compatible with commercial coatings and interior surfaces, thereby expanding the circularity of glass and reducing its dependence on limited recycling routes in the Brazilian context.
