UR  - https://doi.org/10.1201/9781003635222-3
AB  - This chapter analyses key characteristics and implications of quarry wastes (QWs), focusing on regulatory frameworks, research methods, and applications based on waste properties. Initially, global regulations are examined, emphasising environmental impact assessments and standards necessary for safely incorporating QWs into construction across regions, including North America, Europe, Asia, Africa, and South America. Compliance with diverse environmental and safety standards is highlighted to promote sustainable practices. The chapter details research methodologies for characterising QWs, addressing their physical, chemical, and microstructural properties, along with toxicity and safety. Techniques such as granulometric analysis, X-ray fluorescence (XRF), scanning electron microscopy (SEM), and nanoindentation are discussed, illustrating their role in evaluating material suitability. Classifications of QWs based on physical state (solid, dust, slurry), lithological origin (igneous, sedimentary, metamorphic rocks), and mineralogical composition (siliceous, carbonate, ferruginous) are presented. These classifications facilitate identifying suitable applications, from using granite and basalt wastes in high-strength concrete to employing limestone and marble wastes in agriculture and cement production. Finally, the chemical and physical properties of granite, limestone, marble, and basalt wastes are assessed, demonstrating their influence on construction applications. This analysis guides stakeholders towards informed, environmentally responsible reuse of QWs.
