TI  - Development of a standardised framework for communicating confidence in mineral resources and reserves to dimension stone quarries
AU  - Ntiyiso Ally Mahlaule
AU  - Fhatuwani Sengani
PY  - 2026
JO  - The Extractive Industries and Society
DO  - 10.1016/j.exis.2026.101987
UR  - https://doi.org/10.1016/j.exis.2026.101987
AB  - Effective confidence communication in stone resources and reserves enables decision-making, attracts investment, and supports sustainable dimension stone quarry operations. However, a lack of consistency and a dedicated framework for dimension stone quarry operations leads to different interpretations of confidence metrics and transparency problems. The research developed a standardised framework which enables mineral resource and reserve confidence to be communicated through dimension stone quarries, using the Babanana Greyfield area in Limpopo Province, South Africa, as a case study. This study employed a mixed-methods design, combining industry practices, stakeholder consultations (n = 32) participants and statistical analysis using SPSS. The study used descriptive statistics, reliability testing, exploratory factor analysis, regression modelling, and Chi-square tests to examine stakeholder perceptions and to validate the framework. Hypothesis testing was conducted to determine whether there were statistical differences between variables. The results show that participants reached strong agreement about geological assessment (4.5, 0.3), regulatory compliance (4.4), and economic viability (4.3) as the main factors that establish confidence levels. The research revealed a significant link between financial viability and investment objectives, with a correlation coefficient of 0.75 and statistical significance at p < 0.01. The Chi-square analyses yielded values of approximately 1041.13 and 21.25, with p-values below 0.05, indicating substantial statistical differences between the actual and predicted results and providing grounds for rejecting the null hypothesis. The framework demonstrated strong internal reliability (Cronbach’s α = 0.836). The study concluded that a stakeholder-based framework, supported by statistical validation and standardised methods, has the potential to enhance transparency between organisations and enable more effective evaluation of quarry resource expenses, thereby building trust in the reporting process.
