AB  - Pavement deterioration has driven the search for sustainable alternatives materials to improve asphalt concrete performance. This research investigated the use of soybean husk ash (SHA), an agricultural waste rich in calcium oxide, as a partial replacement for conventional mineral filler (granite dust) in wearing courses. Laboratory test was carried out on the constituent material of asphalt concrete in accordance with American Society for Testing Materials and the British Standard specifications. In this study, conventional asphalt mixtures used 100% granite dust as filler, while modified mixtures replaced the filler with 10–50% SHA (at intervals of 10%). Marshall mix design tests were conducted at bitumen contents from 4.5-6.5% (at intervals of 0.5%). The results showed that the optimal bitumen content for both control and modified mixtures was 5.5 % of the total mix weight. Asphalt mix containing 40% SHA achieved an optimum Marshall stability and flow of 10.9KN and 2.51mm respectively. All volumetric properties of the modified mixture remained within specification limits demonstrating its suitability for use. Findings revealed that a durable asphalt mix can be achieved with 40 % SHA and 5.5 % bitumen content is recommended for producing durable, and environmentally sustainable wearing courses with enhanced engineering performance.
