AB  - Waste products from industry, such as red mud, fly ash, and marble dust (MD), have become environmental hazards that must be disposed of or used to reduce environmental pollution and production costs. Researchers’ interest in using these industrial wastes (IWs) as reinforcement to create affordable, lightweight hybrid composites has grown over time. This research provides a thorough analysis of the feasibility of using MD as reinforcement in composites within the same framework. MD is widely utilized in many industries, including the PVC, rubber, plastic, steel, and fertilizer sectors. There are several potential applications for this leftover MD, including the building of homes and composite materials. Polymer matrix composites (PMCs) are more chemically resistant than metals, which makes them appropriate for use in situations where exposure to corrosive environments is a risk. This review also discusses the effect of MD reinforcement on mechanical, tribological, and thermal behaviour of PMCs.
