UR  - https://doi.org/10.22271/27078329.2026.v5.i2a.83
AB  - The earliest experiments with agricultural waste as a concrete aggregate date to the 1970s, yet the specific application of crushed coconut shell in interlocking paving blocks—a product whose performance depends on both compressive strength and surface abrasion resistance—has received limited attention. This research examined the effect of replacing natural coarse aggregate with crushed coconut shell at five levels (0, 10, 20, 30 and 40 % by volume) on the 28-day compressive strength and the Böhme abrasion depth of interlocking concrete paving blocks. A total of 150 blocks (200 mm × 100 mm × 80 mm, I-shaped interlock profile) were cast in steel moulds using a vibration-and-pressure table, with six blocks per replacement level tested at three curing ages (7, 14 and 28 days) for compressive strength and six blocks per level tested at 28 days for abrasion. The concrete mix used CEM I 42.5 N cement at 350 kg/m³, river sand, and 10 mm crushed granite as the control coarse aggregate, with a w/c ratio of 0.42. Crushed coconut shell was oven-dried at 105 °C, sieved to a 4.75-10 mm fraction and pre-soaked for 24 h to limit water absorption during mixing. At 28 days the control blocks reached a mean compressive strength of 35.2 MPa, falling to 33.8, 30.4, 26.7 and 22.1 MPa at 10, 20, 30 and 40 % replacement respectively. All mixes up to 20 % replacement met the EN 1338 Class 3 minimum of 30 MPa for heavy-traffic paving. Abrasion depth rose from 1.82 mm (control) to 3.18 mm at 40 % replacement, but remained below the EN 1338 Class 3 limit of 3.20 mm for every mix. The strength loss followed a near-linear trend (R² = 0.99) and could be predicted by the equation f = 35.4 - 0.33 × replacement (%). An economic comparison showed that at 20 % replacement the material cost per block fell by 8.3 % while still satisfying both the strength and abrasion thresholds for pedestrian and light-traffic applications. These findings indicate that crushed coconut shell can partially replace granite in interlocking paving blocks at up to 20 % without compromising serviceability, offering a route to reducing quarrying pressure and adding value to an agricultural by-product.
