AB  - Nona D. Dronova Doctor of Technical Sciences, Professor Higher School of Folk Arts, St. Petersburg dronovanona@mail.ru Submitted to Zenodo, 2026 Keywords: Yongzheng; monochrome glaze; peacock green; pine needle green; optical microscopy; gemmological analysis; crystalline microstructure; Jingdezhen; Qing dynasty; ceramic authentication; BEINOS method; matte glaze; anorthite; copper colorant; iron colorant Abstract Two imperial monochrome vases from the Yongzheng period (Qing dynasty, 1723–1735), bearing the Da Qing Yongzheng Nian Zhi six-character seal-script reign mark, are subjected to systematic macroscopic and gemmological optical microscopic analysis. The first vase is covered with a deep teal glaze conventionally identified as "Peacock Green" (kongque lü); the second is covered with a subtler blue-green matte glaze identified here as "Pine Needle Green" (songhua lü). Both glazes belong to a category of experimental low-reflectance monochrome wares whose production under the Yongzheng Emperor represented a significant technological advance in controlling crystallisation, surface texture, and colorant chemistry at the imperial kilns of Jingdezhen. Through gemmological optical microscopy at multiple magnifications, distinct crystalline surface microstructures are documented and compared. The observations are situated within the framework of published archaeometric studies on Qing monochrome glazes, crystalline phase formation in high-temperature porcelain glazes, and the non-invasive analytical methods used to characterise coloring agents in imperial ceramics. The study contributes to the author's ongoing series on the authentication and material characterisation of Chinese imperial monochrome porcelain through the BEINOS method. 1. Introduction The Yongzheng Emperor (r. 1723–1735) is widely regarded as the most exacting patron of imperial porcelain in Chinese history. Under the close supervision of the imperial kiln overseer Tang Ying (唐英, 1682–1756), the kilns at Jingdezhen produced an extraordinary range of experimental glazes, many of which were intentionally matte or semi-matte, departing from the brilliant transparent surfaces of the preceding Kangxi period. Among these, a group of deep green wares has long attracted scholarly attention for their unusual optical character: they are neither glossy nor purely opaque but possess a soft, velvety surface suggestive of jade or natural stone. Two colour registers within this green family are examined in the present study. The first, which Chinese connoisseurship tradition associates with the plumage of the peacock (kongque lü, 孔雀綠 in its matte incarnation), presents as a deep blue-teal with low reflectance and a subtle mineral granularity visible to the naked eye and confirmed by microscopy. The second, provisionally identified here as "Pine Needle Green" (songhua lü, 松花綠), is a cooler, more blue-inflected matte green with a finely distributed surface crystalline structure distinct in character
