AB  - The antechamber of Khufu's Great Pyramid at Giza contains the remnants of a sophisticated granite portcullis system comprising three approximately 2 ½ tonne granite slabs guided in vertical wall grooves. Despite the system's central importance to the pyramid's security design, it has (with a few notable exceptions) received surprisingly little rigorous mechanical treatment in the Egyptological literature. This paper applies engineering analysis to the full body of physical evidence — primary survey data from Petrie (1883) and Smyth (1880), photographic evidence, surviving slab fragments documented by Dormion (2004) and Rigano (2015), and dimensionally accurate CAD and 3D-printed physical models of the antechamber — to reconstruct the most probable operational configuration and function of the portcullis mechanism in its archaeological context. A surprisingly large number of previously puzzling architectural features of the antechamber can be explained simultaneously by a single mechanically coherent reconstruction. We confirm — as have others — that the slabs were likely supported during construction by wooden posts seated in the wainscot groove channels; that the grooves extending below floor level argue strongly against the chisel-drop theory; and that simultaneous release of ropes via the "Granite Leaf" (Greaves 1646) would have been mechanically catastrophic. Two independent lines of evidence converge on a roller diameter of approximately 44 cm: the 22 cm height difference between the east and west wainscots, and the D/d ratio required for fiber rope of the diameter the load demands. The unchamfered bore hole edges indicate that ropes were anchored within the slabs rather than run through them, giving eight rope ends per slab, routed over lubricated rollers or logs, redirected northward via a rounded wooden block and a rope-path log, and controlled from the Grand Gallery. Capstan friction analysis yields a total friction multiplier of approximately 1.6× under lubricated conditions, requiring 12–14 people to lower or raise one slab using levers in the Grand Gallery — consistent with the available workspace. Physical model testing establishes that rollers of these dimensions can be installed through the north passage, but only just, and that the otherwise unexplained 2.5 cm dressed-back north face of the Granite Leaf increases the maximum installable log length by approximately 9 cm through a geometric leverage ratio of roughly 3.6:1. The reconstruction yields a specific falsifiable prediction: a cylindrical roller of 44 cm diameter should be installable; one of 50 cm diameter should not. We propose that the Granite Leaf served a security function, positioned to conceal and physically block access to the rope bore holes of the northernmost portcullis slab, and that its division into two stones rather than a single monolith is consistent with a further measure against a toggle attack on the suspended lower leaf. A construction sequence is propos
