AB  - Hybrid composites combine two or more reinforcements in a single matrix, improving mechanical and application-specific performance compared to conventional composites. They are commonly used in industries like aerospace, automotive, construction, etc., due to their lightweight and enhanced mechanical properties. Their properties are said to be drawn from the reinforcements (mainly ceramics). Among ceramic reinforcements, valuable products may be obtained with the use of industrial ceramic wastes, which were generally considered wastes and thereby acted as pollutants. Some of the examples are fly ash (FA), cenosphere, marble dust (MD), red mud, rock powder, and porcelain. This work aims to summarize some popular wastes like MD, FA, red mud, and rock powder and their potential application in product development within the purview of mechanical properties and related applications like tribology, machining, etc. Such a novel step surely aids in sustainable waste management by utilizing them in composite material products that will reduce ecology pollution. The work summarizes that the use of industrial wastes as reinforcements in composite materials improves product performance and the effectiveness of our engineering parts or systems. This leads to a win-win situation for both humans and the environment.
