AB  - In recent years, the construction industry has introduced information and communication technology (ICT)-based construction support systems and remote operation systems to address the shortage of skilled engineers and to enhance safety, quality, and productivity (Ministry of Land, Infrastructure, Transport and Tourism 2023). In port construction, which is conducted under harsh natural conditions, precast and mechanized construction methods are commonly employed; however, some tasks are still performed manually by commercial divers. As the diver workforce ages and the physical demands of the work remain high, fewer young workers are entering the profession, leading to a continued decline in the number of commercial divers. Consequently, ensuring safety and reducing the physical burden associated with diving operations have become urgent challenges. This paper describes the development of a technology for accurately determining the position of an underwater construction machine, enabling the remote execution of a task that has traditionally required commercial divers to manually level the surface of a rubble mound during breakwater construction. To clarify the construction of a breakwater, Fig. 1 presents a typical structural example along with an overview of the construction process. First, crushed natural stone is dumped on the seabed to form a rubble mound. In parallel, reinforced concrete caissons (hollow, box-shaped structures) are fabricated on land or on a barge. Once both processes are completed, the caissons are towed to the construction site and placed on top of the rubble mound (Japan Dredging and Reclamation Engineering Association 2023).
