AB  - This study aims to understand the reasons that led Espírito Santo to assume a leading position in Brazil’s ornamental stone industry, as well as to analyze the specific processes of capital reproduction and appropriation within this sector. The study is guided by the following central question: why did Espírito Santo become the country's main ornamental stone producer? The research demonstrates that, although favorable geological conditions contributed to the development of the activity, the consolidation of the dimension stone industry resulted from the interplay between different actors and historical processes, notably state intervention, business associations, the reconfiguration of international markets, and technological development, particularly in the processing stages carried out locally. From a geographical perspective, the activity is structured around three main microregions – Central South, Northwest, and Greater Vitória – which constitute the core of Espírito Santo’s Local Productive Arrangement (APL). Since the beginning of the twenty-first century, Espírito Santo has not only concentrated the largest share of Brazil’s ornamental stone production but has also assumed a strategic role within the productive chains of other states by processing a significant portion of the stones extracted elsewhere. The findings reveal that capital accumulation in this sector extends beyond the direct exploitation of industrial labor, also manifesting itself through the appropriation of rents associated with landownership and, above all, mineral exploitation rights. These mechanisms enable the capture of portions of socially produced surplus value through specific forms of monopoly and valorization. At the same time, the expansion of the industry intensifies the appropriation of nature, exposing the contradiction between the growing social valorization of natural stones and the destruction of the landscapes from which they originate. By examining these dynamics, this study contributes to a critical understanding of capitalist accumulation and the socio-spatial formation of Espírito Santo.
