AB  - The production of ornamental rocks presents an opportunity for the development of technologies aimed at valorizing their particulate solid waste. Fine waste (< 0.075 mm), when inadequately disposed of, poses an imminent environmental risk. Therefore, developing technologies that enable the economic valorization of such waste can mitigate or even eliminate environmental impacts. This work investigates aspects related to the production of mineral briquettes using waste from a calcareous ornamental rock known as "Bege Bahia," with clay as a binder. An experimental design was conducted to determine the extreme proportions of Bege Bahia rock and clay mixtures, which were pressed into cylindrical forms. The resulting briquettes were tested for dimensional variation, abrasion resistance, and uniaxial and diametral compression strength. The results indicate that the use of clay as a binder improves the mechanical characteristics and abrasion resistance of the briquettes. However, dimensional variation depends on the type of clay used as a binder. The optimal concentration of either 'bentonite clay' or 'red clay' in the mixture for briquette production was found to be 17.5% by mass..
