AB  - Abstract The ornamental stone industry generates large volumes of sludge during its processing. The objective of this research is to evaluate the production of adhesive mortar with partial replacement of fine aggregate by this residue. Physical, chemical, and mineralogical characterizations of the materials were conducted, and formulations were prepared with varying percentages of sludge replacing the fine aggregate. Tests on fresh and hardened adhesive mortars were conducted to verify the product quality. The results indicate a decrease in the consistency index as the sludge replacement percentage increases. Formulations with 100% (dry basis) replacement of sludge showed lower tensile bond strengths and did not meet regulatory standards, whereas formulations with up to 60% residue achieved tensile bond strengths greater than 0.5 MPa, according to the curing conditions. Therefore, there is potential to use industrial solid waste in greater proportions in the production of adhesive mortars, depending on the curing method, thereby minimizing the extraction of non-renewable natural resources as raw materials for this type of construction product.
