AB  - This study investigates the development of precast concrete sandwich panels (PCSPs) incorporating sustainable raw materials, specifically fly ash, lightweight expanded clay aggregates (LECA) and marble waste, to enhance both thermal insulation and structural performance. Five sustainable mixes were experimentally cast and evaluated for axial strength followed by determination of flexural, and shear capacities for the selected optimised PCSP mixes. Also, to study the thermal insulation behaviour of PCSPs; thermal conductivity study was also performed for all PCSPs for better understanding the role of LECA into the developed panels. Before testing, cast PCSPs were cured for 28 days under moist curing. It was inferred that the panel containing marble waste (MW) with steel fibre (1%) demonstrated notable mechanical performance, achieving axial load capacities up to 825.8 kN and flexural strengths of 15.9 kN·m. Incorporating 10% LECA improved ductility with minimal reduction in strength. The use of continuous connectors increased the shear capacity by 9.96%. Thermal analysis revealed U values between 0.3522 and 0.3668 W/m2K, indicating good suitability for energy-efficient applications across diverse climatic zones. Furthermore, the integration of waste materials in the optimum mix resulted in a reduction in CO2 emissions of 17.36% and a decrease in production costs by 6.17%. These findings demonstrate the potential of PCSPs as sustainable, cost-effective, and high-performance solutions for environmentally responsible building construction.
