TI  - Sustainable Landscape Reclamation of Granite Quarries: Environmental Impacts, Restoration Strategies, and Strategic Study Analysis from Guntur, India
AU  - Surendra Babu Gantenapati
AU  - Lavanya Vemula
PY  - 2026
JO  - Zenodo (CERN European Organization for Nuclear Research)
DO  - 10.5281/zenodo.23092152
UR  - https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.23092152
AB  - Abstract— Granite quarrying supplies essential construction materials but causes persistent changes to landform, soil, vegetation, hydrology, biodiversity, and landscape character. This study develops an integrated framework for sustainable quarry reclamation by combining a structured narrative review with an illustrative spatial case study of a granite quarry landscape in Guntur District, Andhra Pradesh, India. The review synthesizes evidence concerning mine-land disturbance, slope stabilization, soil rehabilitation, revegetation, water management, and post-quarry adaptive reuse. The case study integrates field observations and geographic information system analysis of topography, relief, slope, drainage, soil conditions, land use, vegetation indices, and potential surface-water features. Unlike traditional reclamation approaches that often treat engineering stabilization, soil treatment, and revegetation as separate closure measures, the proposed framework connects environmental diagnosis, spatial-priority zoning, ecological restoration, community-oriented ecotourism, and long-term performance monitoring within an adaptive sequence. The findings demonstrate that effective reclamation cannot be reduced to planting or site beautification alone. Stable landform design, runoff and sediment control, recovery or reconstruction of growth media, staged establishment of native vegetation, and a feasible post-quarry use must be planned as coordinated interventions. The Guntur case demonstrates how steep quarry faces, disconnected drainage, exposed rock, waste deposits, and localized water accumulation constrain restoration, while remnant vegetation, surrounding agricultural land, and pit-water storage provide opportunities for ecological recovery, environmental education, and controlled ecotourism. A five-phase framework is proposed comprising site assessment and planning, stabilization and risk control, hydrological and soil recovery, ecological restoration, and ecotourism development with adaptive monitoring. The study contributes a transferable strategic planning structure for landscape architects, quarry operators, regulators, and communities addressing abandoned or closing hard-rock quarries.
