TI  - The Cultural Significance of Lapis-Lazuli in Egypt from the Predynastic through the Early Dynastic Periods
AU  - Thomas Helmut Greiner
PY  - 2026
JO  - TSpace (University of Toronto)
UR  - https://hdl.handle.net/1807/153735
AB  - Lapis-lazuli was a highly prized ornamental stone used by the ancient Egyptians, valued for both its dark blue colour and its foreign origin. Sourced from mines in north-eastern Afghanistan and Tajikistan, lapis-lazuli reached Egypt through long-distance exchange networks that spanned from Egypt across many regions of the wider Ancient Near East. Its rarity, physical quality, and the great efforts to acquire the stone contributed to its perceived value and scholars have characterised lapis-lazuli as a highly sought-after material and item of prestige associated with individuals of high socio-economic status. The present study brings together and analyses all known instances across Egypt during the Predynastic and Early Dynastic Periods (c. 4,000-2,800 BC), focussing particularly on its role within Egypt’s foreign relations and use across Egyptian society in the time of emergent state formation processes. Attestations of lapis-lazuli occurred across Egypt primarily in mortuary contexts and the corpus of lapis-lazuli finds mainly consists of small beads that would have been part of the burial equipment of the deceased. Despite the infrequent and limited quantity of the material, lapis-lazuli has often been associated with high socio-economic status and foreign contacts. This dissertation identifies patterns around the chronological and geographical distribution and the contextual use of lapis-lazuli and argues for a more nuanced interpretation in the stone’s cultural significance. The analysis has revealed variability in how the stone was used in Egyptian society and suggests that access to this material was not exclusively reserved for individuals of high socio-economic status. A more nuanced view of the material may interpret lapis-lazuli beads as signifiers of foreign lands that bestow special knowledge onto the owner of these objects (Stevenson 2009c, 192). The present study also found that lapis-lazuli entered Egypt through several indirect, fluctuating pathways and may have formed part of a broader “prestige-goods economy” present across Egypt and the Ancient Near East during that time (Wengrow 2006, 75-76). It seems therefore likely that the cultural significance of lapis-lazuli was multi-faceted and complex. These findings contribute to broader discussions of Egypt’s foreign relations and use across Egyptian society in the time of emergent state formation processes.
