AB  - The utilization of marble and granite dust (MGD) waste in polymer composites has gained increasing attention due to its environmental and economic benefits. This study aims to investigate the effect of MGD on the physical and mechanical properties of high-impact polystyrene (HIPS) composites for applications such as roof tiles and flooring in residential and industrial buildings. The composites were prepared by incorporating MGD particles (<180 μm) into the HIPS matrix at contents ranging from 0 to 60 wt.% using thermal mixing and hot pressing. Density measurements, water absorption tests, erosion resistance evaluation, three-point bending tests, and ultraviolet (UV) exposure tests were conducted to evaluate the composite performance. The results show that water absorption remained very low, with a maximum increase of 0.11%. Erosion resistance improved with increasing filler content. However, bending strength decreased with increasing filler content and further declined after UV exposure. Therefore, the 60 wt.% MGD provided the best balance between properties, demonstrating the potential of MGD waste as a sustainable filler in HIPS composites.
