AB  - The paper presents the results of an investigation into the effect of waste granite dust on selected properties of cement mortars. The dust was obtained from a stone-processing plant in Rogoźnica, Lower Silesia. Its oxide composition was determined by wavelength-dispersive X-ray fluorescence, showing a high content of SiO2 and Al2O3 and a low content of CaO. This indicates that the material investigated should be considered primarily as a fine-grained mineral component rather than a direct equivalent of cement. A reference mortar was prepared, together with mortars in which 5, 10, 20 and 30% of the cement volume was replaced with granite dust. The consistency of the fresh mortar, the density of the hardened mortar, the flexural tensile strength and the compressive strength after 2 and 28 days were assessed. As the granite dust content increased, the flow diameter decreased and the strength parameters were reduced. After 28 days, the compressive strength decreased from 63.2 to 57.5–40.6 MPa, while the flexural tensile strength decreased from 9.70 to 7.75–6.01 MPa. The CO2 emission reduction, calculated for cement and granite dust, ranged from 4.95 to 29.66%. Based on the proposed descriptive K indices, GD20 showed the most favourable trade-off between estimated CO2 emission reduction and compressive strength loss, whereas GD20 and GD30 exhibited similar relationships for flexural tensile strength.
