AB  - Industrial waste poses a significant challenge for every country, impacting environmental sustainability, public health, and resource management. The utilization of industrial waste as reinforcement in composite materials offers a sustainable approach to improving mechanical properties while reducing environmental impacts. This chapter explores the incorporation of various industrial by-products, such as fly ash, slag, red mud, marble dust, and mill scale, into aluminum matrix composites and other material systems. Different fabrication techniques, including stir casting, powder metallurgy, and friction stir processing, are employed to disperse these waste materials within the matrix, enhancing properties such as hardness, tensile strength, and wear resistance. Each type of reinforcement imparts distinct characteristics to the composite, leading to unique variations in its overall performance. Extensive research worldwide has been conducted to explore the spectrum of composite properties by varying both the type and weight fraction of reinforcements. A case study on Fly Ash/graphite-reinforced aluminum composites is also presented to provide further clarity on the current discussion, offering impactful insights into waste management, applicability, and effectiveness of industrial waste as reinforcement. Finally, this chapter explores the future potential of these sustainable materials, offering insights into how waste utilization aligns with the global shift toward environmentally responsible manufacturing practices.
