AB  - Expansive soils, particularly black cotton soils, pose severe geotechnical challenges owing to their swelling and shrinkage behavior, often resulting in structural damage and costly repairs. Traditional stabilization methods using cement and lime, although effective, are economically and environmentally unsustainable. The findings revealed that marble dust, at 30–35% replacement levels, reduced the liquid limits by up to 33% and improved the California Bearing Ratio (CBR) by approximately 40%, whereas blended approaches with bioenzymes or rice husk ash achieved performance gains exceeding 200%. Iron ore powder and tailings enhance the density and compressive strength, steel slag provides substantial improvements in dry density and moisture control, and agricultural wastes such as rice husk ash and bagasse ash deliver region-specific solutions with CBR gains of 41–127%. Beyond engineering performance, these waste-based stabilizers offer significant cost savings, reduce environmental pollution, and promote sustainable construction practices in the field. Despite this progress, research gaps remain in optimizing blended waste combinations, conducting life-cycle cost analyses, and assessing long-term durability under extreme conditions. The review concludes that industrial waste-based stabilization represents a viable, eco-friendly alternative to conventional methods and holds promise for largescale infrastructure development, particularly in developing countries.
