AB  - Sawdust and marble-processing waste are increasingly generated, creating a need for value-added material routes to reduce waste and produce useful composite materials. This study aimed to fabricate sawdust–marble dust-reinforced epoxy biocomposites and evaluate their physical, mechanical, and tribological behavior. Four formulations were prepared by compression molding using a fixed 40 wt.% epoxy-based binder and different sawdust–marble dust ratios. Density, water absorption, compressive strength, flexural strength, hardness, wear rate, and coefficient of friction were measured, along with comparative dry-sliding wear tests using an in-house pin-on-disc tribometer based on the general ASTM G99 principle. The results showed that the physical, mechanical, and tribological responses of the developed composites depended on filler composition. Among the tested formulations, SD40MD20 showed the most balanced measured performance, with the highest compressive strength, flexural strength, and hardness and the lowest average wear rate among the developed composites. However, this result should be interpreted as a preliminary internal ranking rather than a statistically validated optimum, because the wear tests were single-trial evaluations, some physical-property measurements were single observations, and the in-house tribometer was not benchmarked against a commercial tribometer. The use of sawdust and marble dust represents a potential waste-valorization approach using locally available waste-derived fillers in an epoxy matrix, but no life-cycle assessment or carbon-footprint analysis was conducted. Therefore, Sawdust (SD)/Marble Dust (MD) epoxy composites may be considered candidate waste-derived composite materials, while replicated testing, statistical analysis, particle-size characterization, tribometer benchmarking, microstructural analysis, durability testing, and life-cycle assessment are required before broader application-level claims can be made.
