AB  - To this day, research cannot really explain what images are. The event of pictorial meaning-making, through which a picture emerges from mere matter, thus remains a fascinating mystery, requiring philosophical consideration from the perspective of the picture author, a perspective that has been largely neglected by research to date. The relevance of addressing this question is underlined by the fact that the production of pictures is increasingly becoming an everyday activity and is regarded as a vital skill in both professional and personal spheres. In the context of phenomenological hermeneutics, the present study therefore focuses on the philosophical concepts of a multimodal work aesthetics (Werkästhetik) that uses pictures, in addition to language, as both object and means of inquiry, to examine pictures. To this end, it first draws on the hitherto little-noticed writings of the Romanian German philosopher Walter Biemel (1918–2015), whose reflections on Zeitigung (translated, for example, as temporalization, unfolding and ripening) are based on the thinking of Martin Heidegger (1889–1976). Secondly, the studies of Gottfried Boehm (* 1942) are of significant relevance for this study. In addition, other relevant research approaches are included, whereby the visual arts represent an important field of experimentation for the picture creation, while statements on artistic creation emphasize the need to overcome the anthropocentric perspective of the picture author. Following a word-field-oriented examination of the barely translatable vocabulary, which spans the continuum between general and specialized language, it becomes apparent that the event of pictorial meaning-making can be summarized in a single word, Zeitigung, in a manner specific to the German language. This way, complex questions of agency as extrinsic Zeitigen and intrinsic Sich-Zeitigen are addressed, without neglecting aspects such as Zeitigung as ‘bringing about’ and ‘maturing’. In the context of the distinction made between multimodal research publications and approaches, the question arises as to the role of the picture in its investigation and how the event of picture creation is represented and analyzed by means of language and static images. Instead of the human-made objects that Boehm uses in his drawing experiment on the primal scene of the iconic, the mere thing (bloßes Ding) or natural thing (Naturding) is proposed as the starting point for analysis, based on Heidegger’s concept of the thing (Ding). Using the example of natural stone shaped by the forces of nature (das Eigenwüchsige), the event in which a three-dimensional picture or sculpture emerges from amorphous material is thematized. The concept of the yet-to-be-done (das Zu-Zeitigende) is then formulated to explicitly address that which has not yet been done (das noch nicht Gezeitigte). From the perspective of the picture author, this constitutes the focal point of interest. At the same time, questions arise conc
