AB  - The relevance of this study. The stone-processing industry in Korostyshiv and its surrounding areas is a key revenue-generating sector for local community economies. However, there is a lack of information regarding the industry's history and its early development. Therefore, the aim of this study is to trace the origins of the stone-processing industry in Korostyshiv, identify the primary factors behind its emergence, and locate the earliest examples of its products. The research has identified the founders of industrial production and specific product samples, as well as determined their role in the industry's development. The purpose of this article is to determine the contribution of the Italian families Rizzolatti, Buzetti, and others to the development of the stone processing industry in Ukraine. Conclusions. The development of the stone-processing industry in the Korostyshiv region was driven primarily by the area's natural resources. At the same time, the construction of a section of the Brest-Litovsk Highway through Korostyshiv attracted craftsmen who recognized the potential for establishing their own stone-processing businesses in an environment free from competition. Beyond establishing a full-fledged industry in Korostyshiv—one that became a key source of budget revenue, contributed to urban improvements, and raised the local population's standard of living—the Rizzolatti family also produced goods that were competitive across the entire empire, including steps, balustrades, windowsills, washbasins, and stone slabs. Moreover, they set a trend for exquisite stone funerary sculpture—crafted not only from local stone but also from imported marble. By employing local residents, Rizzolatti, Buzetti, and others enabled the community to master professional stone-working techniques. This represents their greatest contribution: by training two generations of men in the craft, they introduced the locals to a new professional vocation. This activity eventually evolved into cooperative production, which the Soviet authorities later nationalized.
