AB  - The utilization of quarry waste (QW) as a sustainable alternative in cementitious composites offers an opportunity to decrease cement consumption and mitigate industrial by-product disposal. This study examines the influence of mechanical activation of granite powder waste (GPW) into mechanically processed granite powder (GPM) on hydration behaviour, microstructural evolution, and mechanical properties of cementitious pastes. Optimisation of the mechanical activation process was also performed, taking into account three different process parameters (grinding time, grinding type and steel ball type). A comprehensive methodology integrating thermogravimetric analysis (TGA), scanning electron microscopy (SEM), and X-ray micro-computed tomography (µCT) was employed to assess hydration kinetics, porosity, and phase distribution. Functional properties of GPM-modified composites such as pull-off tensile strength, sulphate attack, chloride penetration or freeze-thaw resistance were also investigated. Findings indicate that 2-hour, wet mechanical activation leads to the best GPM impact on composites, substantially lowers porosity (by 41 %), and enhances volume density, resulting in a 4.5 % and 4.9 % increase in compressive strength at 7 and 28 days, respectively. However, TGA analysis confirms that both GPW and GPM remain chemically inert, exhibiting a hydration degree significantly lower than that of cementitious phases. Isothermal calorimetry further demonstrates a heat release reduction of up to 20 %, suggesting potential advantages in minimizing thermal cracking and shrinkage in large-scale concrete structures. From an industrial standpoint, mechanically processed QW can function as a viable cement substitute at 20 % replacement without significantly compromising mechanical performance, particularly in non-structural and precast concrete applications.
