TI  - Breathing Easy? – Understanding Workers’ Lung Health in Australia
AU  - K. Greeneberg
PY  - 2025
JO  - American Journal of Respiratory and Critical Care Medicine
DO  - 10.1164/ajrccm.2025.211.abstracts.a6324
UR  - https://doi.org/10.1164/ajrccm.2025.211.abstracts.a6324
AB  - Abstract Rationale: Occupational lung diseases (OLDs) describes a range of lung conditions caused by breathing in dusts, fumes, gases and other hazardous agents in the workplace. OLDs are typically incurable, although they are entirely preventable. There has been a resurgence of OLDs both in Australia and internationally, including the recent epidemic of silicosis. Australian governments have taken steps, including the world-leading decision to prohibit the use and importation of engineered stone products. Despite new regulations, there remains an under recognition of OLD's and their risk factors amongst high-risk workplaces in Australia. Therefore, increasing workers knowledge of disease, risk factors, and control measures are needed to reduce the incidence of OLDs. Further, education will support positive behaviour change, improving the lung health of Australian workers. Method: To build awareness of OLDs and their risk factors, Lung Foundation Australia developed the Healthy Lungs at Work Quiz. The Quiz collected information on industry, exposures, workplace safety practices, respiratory symptoms and doctor engagement. The Quiz output was downloadable and encouraged discussion of information with a doctor. Participants were given the option to receive follow-up, including individualised health information and resources to support medical appointments. The Quiz commenced during Lung Foundation Australia's National Silicosis Prevention and Awareness Campaign, launched in October 2023 and again in October 2024. To reach culturally and linguistically diverse workers, of which there are a large proportion working in at-risk industries, the Quiz was translated into Vietnamese, Simplified Chinese and Arabic, Punjabi, Nepali and Spanish. Results: During the campaigns in 2023 and 2024, the Quiz was completed by 6,342 Australian workers. The results report silica dust, dust from hard metals and gases or vapours as the top three exposures. Building/construction and mining were the most reported industries. Eighty-eight percent of workers had not spoken to their doctor about the workplace or symptoms, an increase from 78% in the inaugural quiz in 2023. Concerningly, breathless/shortness of breath, a blocked and/or runny nose and a new, changed or persistent cough were amongst the most reported symptoms. Conclusions: The rich data and results of this Quiz will continue to build evidence on the OLD climate in Australia, with a focus on risk factors. It also provides a snapshot of the experience of Australian workers, highlighting their exposures, industries, and current respiratory health. The findings will help inform key stakeholders, including government and industry, on how and where to take action.
