AB  - The concrete industry is evolving toward sustainability to reduce reliance on natural resources, mitigate the environmental impacts of concrete production, and lower carbon dioxide emissions. This study explores the feasibility of incorporating seashell powder (SSP) and glass powder (GP) as partial replacements for cement at 10%, combined with marble and granite waste (MA and GA) at varying levels (0%, 10%, 20%, and 30%) to replace sand in the flowable sand concrete (FSC). The mechanical properties were assessed through compressive strength, dynamic modulus of elasticity, and ultrasonic pulse velocity (UPV) at 7, 28, and 90 days. The optimal mix, containing 10% SSP, 10% GP, and 20% MA + 20% GA, exhibited the highest improvements in mechanical properties. The compressive strength increased by 37.69% at 7 days, 29.55% at 28 days, and 29.55% at 90 days compared to the control mix. The dynamic modulus of elasticity showed enhancements of 17.31%, 13.81%, and 13.83% at the same intervals, while the UPV improved by 16.81%, 12.64%, and 11.04%, respectively. These results demonstrate that the integration of SSP, GP, MA, and GA can significantly enhance the performance of FSC while promoting sustainability through the use of recycled materials.
