AB  - Stabilization of soil is performed to modify soil characteristics to improve its engineering performances. This research is based on an intention to probe the impact of marble dust (MD) upon stabilization of expansive soil such as black cotton soil. In this analysis black cotton soil taken as the soil specimen, is characterized by high compressibility, high shrinkage, high moisture content and high expansion on drying including deep cracks and excessive swelling on wetting. Black cotton soil exists in various parts of Odisha. In Odisha province, a site is selected and soil samples are collected for determining engineering and index properties. While selecting site, the soil is visually inspected and soil samples are collected from areas in which the soil during its dry condition has deep and wide cracks. The index properties of soil samples taken were classified as expansive soil (black cotton soil) and the liquid limit (LL) is above 35% and plasticity index (PI) is above 20%. Various techniques used for improving the black cotton soil are mostly uneconomical nowadays. For the improvement of black cotton soil, we use marble dust which is oppressing the natural environment. Therefore, utilizing these wastes in order to stabilize the black cotton soil would be the best alternative. The marble dust used in the present study was obtained by cutting of the Makrana marble. A radical increase was attained in shrinkage limit (SL) from 18.35% to 34.72%. The LL and PI were decreased gradually from 52.20% to 38.32% and 34.42% to 20.62% respectively. The inclusion of marble dust improved its maximum dry density (MDD) from 1.77 to 1.804.Results divulged that an inclusion of marble dust pronounces a favorable outcome on California bearing ratio (CBR) values for both soaked and unsoaked conditions. CBR value increased from 6% to 8.58% on inclusion of 20% marble dust. It also revealed that the inclusion of marble dust in the expansive soil gradually decreases the hydraulic conductivity from 1.38x10 −2 cm/sec to 8.73x10 −3 m/sec. A descending trend of settlement was found from the consolidation test from 0.309cm to 0.187cm. Therefore, from the above results, it was proven that marble dust is economically an alternative to the use of ordinary Portland cement and chemical stabilizers and is a fruitful way for the treatment of expansive soil.
