TI  - Hospitalization Costs and Payors for Silicosis Among Engineered Stone Countertop Workers in California
AU  - Jane C. Fazio
AU  - Kathryn Gibb
AU  - Amy Heinzerling
AU  - Jennifer Flattery
AU  - Sheiphali A. Gandhi
AU  - Beth A. Glenn
AU  - Kristin J. Cummings
AU  - Russell G. Buhr
AU  - Ninez A. Ponce
AU  - A. Bustamante
PY  - 2025
JO  - American Journal of Respiratory and Critical Care Medicine
DO  - 10.1164/ajrccm.2025.211.abstracts.a6311
UR  - https://doi.org/10.1164/ajrccm.2025.211.abstracts.a6311
AB  - Abstract Rationale:Silicosis, a preventable and progressive fibrotic occupational lung disease, has become increasingly prevalent among engineered stone countertop workers globally, though the financial cost has not been estimated. This study uses administrative hospital discharge data to estimate the financial cost of hospitalizations for countertop workers with silicosis in California. Methods:We used California's Department of Health Care Access and Information's Patient Discharge Data. Our sample included hospitalizations with an International Classification of Disease (ICD)-10 code designating silicosis (J62.8) and occupation as a stone fabricator, determined by manual review of medical records and telephone interview follow-up. Cost per hospitalization was calculated by multiplying the charge for the hospital stay by the hospital's annual cost-to-charge ratio, then reported in 2024 dollars using the national medical consumer price index, and visits with missing charge data were excluded. Results:A total of 85 hospitalizations for 37 patients were included from 2006-2022, with a total cost of $3,778,117. The median age of the patients at the time of hospitalization was 48 years [range 26-68 years], all were male, and 77 (91%) were Hispanic. Total annual cost increased substantially in 2022 (Figure 1). The median cost per hospitalization was $27,000 [range $5,550- $560,000], and median length of stay was 8 days [range 0-65 days]. Expected payors were: Medicaid (n=55, 65%) covering $1.8 million, private insurance (n=16, 19%) covering $1.1 million, Medicare (n=5, 6%) covering $288,717, self-pay/other (n=5, 6%) covering $486,896 and worker's compensation (n=4, 5%) covering $177,766. Regionally, hospitalizations occurred most often in Los Angeles (n=45, 52%), followed by other areas of southern California (n=19, 22%), San Francisco Bay Area (n=14, 17%), and central California (n=7, 8%). Patients had a median of one hospitalization [range 1-11]. Conclusion:The financial cost of hospitalizations due to silicosis, a preventable disease, among engineered stone workers in California from 2006-2022 was nearly $4 million, the highest in 2022. Hospitalizations were predominantly paid for by public insurance rather than worker's compensation, despite the cause of the disease being occupational. Our study was limited to hospitalizations for silicosis identified by ICD-10 codes, and therefore likely underestimates hospitalizations, as misdiagnosis is common. We expect costs to increase, as data from the California Department of Public Health show silicosis cases and concomitant lung transplantations among countertop workers have increased substantially since 2022. Our findings highlight the need for further investigation to understand why workers’ compensation covered only a small proportion of hospitalizations.
