AB  - 1. Silicosis cases associated with unsafe cutting of high silica-containing artificial stone (AS) are increasing worldwide. Disease-relevant models are required to provide insights into the consequences of exposure to respirable crystalline silica from AS. 2. To establish cell- and tissue-based models reflective of the silica load and secretome in silicosis BAL. 3. BAL from an Australian cohort (silicosis n=22, control n=6) was analysed for birefringent silica particles in macrophages and for inflammatory mediators (92 cytokine Olink® panel). THP-1 macrophagess and PCLS (containing structural cells and resident macs) were treated with different types of silica to measure uptake and cytokine release. 4. Silica load in BAL (present in up to 37% of total macs, 1-52 particles/mac) was associated with worse disease severity. Sixteen differentially expressed proteins detected in silicosis BAL included IL-1α, IL-18, MCP-1, -2, -3 (all p<0.05). Silica uptake was visualised in both THP1 macs (100 ug/ml Sigma silica, present in ~ 25% cells) and alveolar macs within PCLS (increased in rolling culture) without loss of viability. Silica treatment increased IL-1 secretion from THP-1 cells (3-fold) and IL-6 secretion from PCLS (%control: Sigma 232+/-45; DQ-12 454+/-161; Alpha Quartz 388+/-108, all n=5). 5. Comprehensive characterization of BAL from AS-asssocated silicosis has provided real-world exposure insights for developing THP-1 macrophage and PCLS models. These can be utilised to interrogate differential uptake and inflammatory consequences of dust exposure from current commercial AS products and from lower silica alternatives.
