AB  - Introduction: This study evaluated the effect of waterproofing on the staining and chemical resistance of ornamental stones, focusing on durability and aesthetic preservation. Samples of Preto São Gabriel and Cinza Corumbá granites, treated and untreated with commercial waterproofing, were analyzed. They were subjected to staining and chemical agents according to ABNT NBR 10545-14 and NBR 16596 standards. The results demonstrated distinct behaviors between the lithotypes, indicating that the effectiveness of the waterproofing depends on the mineralogical and structural characteristics of each stone. The treatment proved more effective for oil stains on Preto São Gabriel and for oil and coffee stains on Cinza Corumbá. However, when exposed to strong chemical agents, such as hydrochloric acid, both granites exhibited significant color changes, regardless of the product's application. The conclusion is that the choice of waterproofing agent should consider the stone type and the exposure environment to optimize performance and durability. The study contributes to improving conservation and maintenance practices for ornamental stone, promoting more sustainable and technically sound use of these materials in civil construction. Objective: This study aimed to evaluate the behavior of waterproofed and non-waterproofed ornamental rocks in staining and chemical attack tests, focusing on durability and aesthetic preservation. Samples of two different types of stone were treated with a commercial waterproofing agent and subjected to staining and chemical agents, simulating typical use conditions. Theoretical Framework: The ornamental stone sector plays an important role in the construction industry, being widely used in cladding, flooring, and facades due to its durability and aesthetic value. Technological characterization tests, such as resistance to chemical attack and staining, are essential to predict good performance and durability. The application of waterproofing agents reduces liquid absorption and, together with the appropriate selection of material and treatment, ensures greater durability and aesthetic preservation of the stones in various applications. Method: This study used commercially available stones, with and without waterproofing, to compare their performance. The stain test followed an adaptation of ABNT NBR 10545-14, exposing the samples to various agents for 24 hours. Resistance to chemical attack followed ABNT NBR 16596, with exposure to reagents for 24 to 96 hours. The surfaces were inspected before and after the tests to identify changes, allowing us to assess the effect of waterproofing on the stone durability. Results and Discussion: Stain and chemical resistance analyses demonstrated distinct behaviors between Preto São Gabriel granite and Cinza Corumbá granite, revealing that the response to waterproofing treatments is not uniform across the materials. In chemical etching tests, both lithotypes were more affected by strong acid
