AB  - Abstract Quartzite is an ornamental metamorphic rock that has attracted interest in the construction industry in recent years. Its extraction leads to significant environmental impacts on various scales, along with occupational hazards for workers involved in mining activities. The extraction process generates substantial material losses, producing approximately 80% waste. To utilize quartzite waste from mining activities in Ouro Preto city, Minas Gerais - Brazil, compressed stabilized bricks were developed from a mixture of a base material and stabilizing agents Portland cement. The quartzite waste underwent physical characterization through granulometric composition and specific gravity, and chemical and mineralogical analysis through XRF, XRD, and loss on ignition. Preliminary tests were conducted using cylindrical specimens submitted to compressive strength and water absorption tests. Two mixtures with ratios 1:0.075:0.14 and 1:0.10:0.14 (quartzite:cement:water) were tested. Full-size bricks of both mixes achieved the minimum compressive strength of 2 MPa and both mixtures presented water absorption below 20%. Therefore, the mixtures met the requirements of Brazilian Standard NBR 8492:2012. The result was an economically attractive material that does not require firing and has the potential to mitigate some of the environmental issues associated with improper quartzite waste disposal.
