AB  - Electron microscopy attracted enormous scientific attention because it involves a beam of electrons as the source of illumination due to which it produced extremely high spatial resolution images about 0.01 nm and magnification up to 300000X which makes it the most promising and powerful tool for the characterization of a wide range of material in the field of nanoscience, nanotechnology, and biomedical research. The properties of material modify as their size approaches the nanoscale as a result method for precise characterization of nanomaterial has become indispensable. During the past few years, different types of electron microscopy such as TEM, SEM, FESEM, and STEM are used to study the morphological characteristics including shape, size, strain, phase, and structure of nanomaterial. However, particularly scanning electron microscopy (SEM) as well as EDS and transmission electron microscopy (TEM) are widely considered as gold standard for many materials characterization. The EDS results reveal the elemental composition of industrial MW (microwave) materials as follows: calcium at 43.13%, magnesium at 16.29%, silicon at 20.42%, aluminum at 8.63%, potassium at 5.07%, and iron at 2.79%. Additionally, trace amounts of other elements such as tin, phosphorus, cadmium, and lead were detected. Several segments of marbles with unusual powder or slurry waste have been generated by the industries. Millions of tons of waste generated by the marble polishing, cutting, processing, and crushing industries are released in India every year. Environmental problems can come from directly exposing waste to the environment. Consequently, several countries continue to study ways for recycling waste materials. Marble slurry has an impact on the fertility of the soil, plants, and humans. When waste is dumped on land, the wind carries particles into the atmosphere, causing air pollution in the surrounding area. An important environmental management tool for getting environmental sustainability is the utilization of waste marble as a resource. The comprehensive research includes a thorough review of the available research on the use of waste marble in various building materials, including cement, concrete-based composite materials, and bricks. The presented study covers material characterization and morphological study of waste marbles to conclude the wide applications.
