TI  - Surgical Outcomes for Lung Transplant Patients With Silicosis at a Center in Southern California
AU  - S.S. Gaboyan
AU  - Rebecca Robinson
AU  - K. Villegas
AU  - B.J. Lee
AU  - Aarya Kafi
AU  - Christine M. Lin
AU  - Gordon Yung
AU  - Catherine Gaissert
AU  - C. Malles
AU  - A. Maria Gloria
AU  - Kamyar Afshar
AU  - Eugene M. Golts
PY  - 2025
JO  - American Journal of Respiratory and Critical Care Medicine
DO  - 10.1164/ajrccm.2025.211.abstracts.a1873
UR  - https://doi.org/10.1164/ajrccm.2025.211.abstracts.a1873
AB  - Abstract Rationale: Silicosis, a lung disease caused by silica dust inhalation, has resurged among engineered countertop workers in Southern California, primarily affecting Hispanic immigrants. Silicosis associated with artificial stone masonry is more aggressive than that from natural stone masonry due to higher silica content. As a result, lung transplants for silicosis due to engineered stone have increased. These surgeries have been reported to be lengthy and arduous due to pleuritis, and little is known about the associated outcomes for these patients. This study examines perioperative and one-year post-operative outcomes for silicosis patients who received a lung transplant relative to non-silicosis interstitial lung disease (ILD) patients. Methods: Using the electronic health record and United Network for Organ Sharing database, we identified patients with silicosis and non-silicosis ILD who underwent lung transplant at UC San Diego Health from 2019 to 2023. Data on patient demographics and history was collected and analyzed using descriptive statistics. Results: Among 85 patients, 7 (7.6%) had silicosis, and 78 (92.4%) had non-silicosis ILDs. Silicosis patients were younger (mean age 43 years, SD = 10) compared to non-silicosis ILD patients (mean age 60 years, SD = 7; p < 0.001). The mean ischemic times for left and right donor lungs in silicosis patients were significantly longer (417 minutes, SD = 128; 370 minutes, SD = 133) than in non-silicosis ILD patients (286 minutes, SD = 92; 229 minutes, SD = 79; p < 0.01 and p < 0.005, respectively). Post-transplant, there were no significant differences in days on mechanical ventilation (silicosis: 2.0 days, SD = 0.6; non-silicosis ILD: 2.8 days, SD = 3.0; p > 0.05), ECMO use (silicosis: 0 days; non-silicosis ILD: 0.2 days, SD = 1.2; p > 0.05), revision surgery (silicosis: 0%; non-silicosis ILD: 1.3%; p > 0.05), or dialysis rates (silicosis: 0%; non-silicosis ILD: 1.3%; p > 0.05). One-year survival was 100% in silicosis patients compared to 84.7% in non-silicosis ILD patients (p > 0.05). Conclusion: Lung transplant candidates with silicosis received donor lungs with greater ischemic times relative to those with non-silicosis ILD, likely due to lengthier procedure involving pleuritis and adhesion. Despite these challenges, their post-operative outcomes, including survival, were comparable to those of non-silicosis ILD patients. Lung transplantation remains a viable option for patients with end-stage silicosis from artificial stone exposure.
