UR  - https://doi.org/10.1080/15320383.2025.2574272
AB  - This research advances the sustainability of real onshore oil-based drill cuttings remediation by introducing a new blended binder using metakaolin (MK), brick powder (BP), marble powder (MP), glass powder (GP), natural pozzolan (POZ), and blast furnace slag (BFS) as a cement substitute for stabilizing chlorides (Cl−) and total petroleum hydrocarbons (TPH). The study investigates key factors, including cuttings content, addition type, curing duration, and the leaching test methodology. The findings revealed that mixtures incorporating MK, POZ, and BFS achieved higher compressive strength. Furthermore, metakaolin demonstrated significant improvements in early- and later-age strength, with 28-day gains of 60.42%, 46.05%, and 35.22% for MK, POZ, and BFS matrices, respectively, reaching 51.36%, 49.24%, and 47.23% after 90 days. Leaching tests indicated effective chloride stabilization and substantial TPH reductions, with 28-day TPH leaching decreased by 99.55%, 98.41%, and 98.22% for MK, POZ, and BFS, respectively, while chloride reductions reached 66.76%, 65.35%, and 64.93%. Microstructural analyses of the MK-based matrix confirmed enhanced formation of calcium silicate hydrate (CSH), supporting the observed improvements in mechanical strength and leaching resistance. This approach not only reduces cement consumption but also offers a practical solution for managing oil exploration waste, enabling its safe reuse and contributing to environmental sustainability.
