AB  - Manailo. In this article, the author analyzes the contribution of Fedir Manailo to the field of monumental art. Drawing upon ma- terials from the Zakarpattia Regional State Ar- chive, archival documents, and sketches of monumental decorations preserved in the col- lections of the artist’s memorial house- museum, the study reconstructs the chronology of his monumental oeuvre, traces the genesis of his stylistic solutions and technical experi- ments, and examines the transformation of the thematic and figurative content of his works. Вісник 18 ЗАКАРПАТСЬКА АКАДЕМІЯ МИСТЕЦТВ 57 The article highlights the factors that shaped the artist’s interest in monumental art, particu- larly his studies at the Higher School of Applied Arts in Prague, and analyzes the influence of the socio-cultural processes of the second half of the twentieth century on the development of his monumental practice. Special attention is given to both realized and unrealized projects for the monumental decoration of public buildings in Zakarpattia and other regions of Ukraine, executed in the techniques of mosaic, sgraffito, stained glass, ceramic painting, stone carving, and wood carving. The study traces the main stages in the development of the artist’s monumental work, from his first postwar sketches created within the framework of official Soviet aesthetics to the mature decorative-monumental composi- tions of the 1960s and 1970s, in which his ar- tistic individuality and aspiration for experi- mentation became increasingly evident. Partic- ular emphasis is placed on the monumental decoration of bus shelters, clubs, houses of cul- ture, public institutions, and other architectural objects that became important spheres of the artist’s creative realization. The article reveals the distinctive features of Fedir Manailo’s artistic language, character- ized by a synthesis of folk imagery and modern plastic techniques, a tendency toward decora- tiveness, generalized forms, stylization, and the active use of ornamental motifs. The transfor- mation of the thematic range of his monumen- tal works is analyzed, from ideologically condi- tioned subjects to compositions inspired by folk culture, historical memory, folklore image- ry, and regional traditions. The innovative character of certain artistic solutions is noted, particularly his use of natural stone in mosaic compositions, the combination of heterogene- ous materials, and the introduction of graphic elements into the structure of monumental panels. The study demonstrates that monumental art occupies an important place in Fedir Manailo’s creative legacy and constitutes an in- tegral component of the development of Zakarpattian art in the second half of the twen- tieth century. It was precisely in his monumen- tal projects that his aspiration toward the syn- thesis of art and architecture, his search for new forms of artistic expression, and his inter- est in the organization of public space were most fully realized. The research m
