TI  - The Role of Particle Size in Engineered Stone Induced Lung Cell Responses
AU  - Chandnee Ramkissoon
AU  - Yong Song
AU  - Seiha Yen
AU  - Katherine A. Southam
AU  - Sharyn E. Gaskin
AU  - Dino Pisaniello
AU  - Graeme R. Zosky
PY  - 2025
JO  - American Journal of Respiratory and Critical Care Medicine
DO  - 10.1164/ajrccm.2025.211.abstracts.a5092
UR  - https://doi.org/10.1164/ajrccm.2025.211.abstracts.a5092
AB  - Abstract Rationale: The surge in silicosis cases among engineered stone (ES), also known as artificial stone, workers is a global health disaster. This has been linked to the high levels of respirable crystalline silica (RCS) that workers processing ES may be exposed to. However, recent clinical studies have shown that ES-associated silicosis develops faster and is more progressive than traditional forms of silicosis, suggesting that physicochemical properties unique to ES dusts, including fine particle size, may be contributing to toxicity and disease severity in this occupational group. This study aimed to understand how different ES particle size fractions (PM10, PM2.5 and PM1.0) influenced inflammatory and cytotoxic responses in lung cells. Methods: In a custom-built test chamber, we cut 4 ES samples using an angle grinder, simulating real-world stone processing tasks. During cutting, dust emissions were drawn through a 10-stage cascade impactor (MiniMoudi 135-10B) that separated the particles into 10 size fractions from 10 µm down to <56 nm. To assess the effect of these size-fractionated dusts on lung cell response, alveolar epithelial cells (A549; ATCC) and macrophages (THP-1; ATCC) were exposed to 200 ug/L of particles. After 24 hours of exposure cytotoxicity, by lactate dehydrogenase (LDH) assay and production of cell relevant inflammatory cytokines (ELISA), was assessed. Results: Relative to control, all ES samples caused cytotoxicity and increased cytokine production (A549 – IL-6, IL-8; THP-1 – IL-1β, TNF-α, IL-8) in both cell lines (p < 0.05). There was evidence of particle size dependent responses in A549 cells for two out of the four ES samples for cytotoxicity, IL-6 and IL-8 with a third sample showing particle size dependent impacts on IL-8 production (p < 0.05 for all comparisons). For THP-1 cells, all of the ES samples showed particle size dependent impacts in cytotoxicity or at least one of the cytokines that was measured. In this cell type the largest effects, particularly in IL-1β production, were observed for the smaller size fractions (PM2.5, PM1) which were also greater than the response induced by standard silica (p < 0.05). Conclusions: The cytotoxic and inflammatory impact of ES particles were clearly particle size dependent. The variability in the magnitude of the size dependency between samples suggested that other physicochemical properties of ES dusts, e.g. silica and metals content or morphology of particles, were also important factors. Full physicochemical characterisation of ES dusts is therefore necessary to understand disease pathogenesis among workers.
