AB  - Recycling industrial waste materials such as granite powder (GP), ground granulated blast furnace slag (GGBFS) and sintered red mud (SRM) provides sustainable, cost-efficient and low-energy consumption binders beneficial to the building industry. This research determined the strength of one-part GGBFS and GP binders substituted with SRM (0% − 20%). The tests included compressive strength, XRD, FTIR, TG, SEM-EDS, thermal resistance, HCl, H2SO4, MgSO4 attack and water absorption. The results showed that hydrotalcite, C-S-H, nyerereite and calcite improved strength, and an optimal strength of 35.58 MPa was obtained at 28d with 5% SRM substitution. Above 5% SRM substitution, the excessive calcite, limited reactive silicates and aluminates led to strength decline. The decomposition of hydration products at elevated temperature exposure resulted in strength reduction in SRM samples. At 28d HCl exposure, the strength of SRM samples up to 15% performed better than the initial strength. The strength of samples subjected to 28d H2SO4 showed that 5% and 15% SRM had lower strength than the initial strength. The strength of 10% and 15% SRM samples performed better than the initial strength at 28d MgSO4 exposure while the strength of all the SRM samples immersed in water recorded improved strength at 28d.
