AB  - The growth in the road sector and increased traffic demand more durable roads. In the 1980s, shortages of bitumen and aggregates led to alternative materials. Aggregate production depletes resources, harming the environment and economy, making alternative byproducts crucial. Commonly recycled materials include plastic waste, scrap tires, foundry sand, fly ash, marble dust, recycled concrete, RAP, and steel slag. Research on recycling seeks continuous improvements. Despite rejuvenation additives, RAP is effective in road construction. Plastic waste holds great potential, with millions of tonnes produced and thermosetting plastics needing alternative uses due to non-recyclability. Research focuses on polyethylene and PVC for asphalt modification. Polymers like PE, PP, PET, PS, and PVC are commonly recycled. Developing durable composite materials is vital for civil engineering. Studies use microscopy to evaluate plastic in concrete and examine lifecycle impacts for environmental clarity. Sustainable construction requires understanding recycled aggregate asphalt. This study promotes reclaimed asphalt for green infrastructure, comparing it to conventional asphalt, optimizing aggregate proportions, and assessing environmental and economic benefits. Mechanical properties (strength, stiffness, deformation resistance) and durability (aging, moisture damage, cracking resistance) will be tested. Understanding recycled aggregate asphalt is key to sustainable construction and green infrastructure.
