JO  - International Journal of Apllied Mathematics
DO  - 10.12732/ijam.v38i4s.1949
UR  - https://doi.org/10.12732/ijam.v38i4s.1949
AB  - The construction industry's reliance on Portland cement has led to significant environmental concerns, with cement production accounting for approximately 7-8% of global CO₂ emissions. Concurrently, the marble processing industry generates substantial non-biodegradable waste, posing serious disposal challenges. This paper presents a comprehensive analysis of utilizing waste marble powder (WMP) as a partial cement replacement in concrete, synthesizing findings from recent experimental studies published between 2023 -Jan 2025. Through systematic evaluation of mechanical properties, durability performance, and environmental impacts, we demonstrate that WMP incorporation at optimal levels (5-10%) enhances both compressive and flexural strengths while improving durability characteristics. Key findings indicate that 5-10% cement replacement with WMP increases compressive strength by 5-25% and flexural strength by up to 20%, attributed primarily to the filler effect and improved particle packing density. Higher replacement levels (15-20%) lead to strength reductions due to cement dilution and limited pozzolanic activity. Durability assessments reveal enhanced resistance to acid attack, reduced chloride ion permeability, and improved water tightness at optimal replacement levels. Life cycle assessments demonstrate 12-20% reductions in global warming potential, establishing WMP as a viable sustainable construction material. This paper provides quantitative benchmarks for optimal replacement levels, performance metrics, and identifies research gaps for future investigation.
