AB  - Abstract Natural stone industry wastewater causes water pollution to the Jamblang River, the main water source of 2,200 hectares of rice fields in the Jamblang Irrigation Area, Palimanan District, Cirebon Regency, Indonesia. This water pollution can result in a decrease in rice productivity due to contamination of water sources. Therefore, efforts are needed to improve water quality so that polluted water can be reused for rice field irrigation. Reusing wastewater is a solution to facing the problem of increasing water demand and water scarcity, it also can make a significant contribution for controlling pollutants in the environment. In this research, a treatment prototype was built to improve the quality of natural stone industry wastewater, then it can be reused for rice field irrigation. The treatments consists of initial treatment in the form of a coarse filter using bamboo and a fine filter using a mosquito ram, primary treatments with a sand trap, and secondary and tertiary treatment using artificial wetlands. The results of the research show that based on 15 parameters, the efficiency of reducing pollutants in improving water quality is between 8% - 99%, especially for the TSS, COD, Total Phosphate, and Fecal Coliform parameters that can meet class II water quality requirements for irrigation.
