AB  - The development of 3D-printed mortars formulated with marble powder in combination with cement or lime is an incremental step towards sustainable and versatile construction materials. Marble powder, a by-product of the stone industry, can be exploited for its aesthetic appeal and sustainable attributes, reducing the natural sand rate. By incorporating it into 3D-printed mortars, this research seeks to advance the state-of-the-art in architectural design, contributing to a circular economy while reducing the environmental impact of construction materials. The synergy between marble powder and traditional binders is explored, focusing on optimising material compositions and printing parameters. Some of the developed mortars were characterised by mechanical strength and water absorption tests to assess the impact of marble powder on these properties. The printed elements exhibited distinctive visual characteristics due to the marble&s;s unique texture and colour patterns. Through systematic experimentation and analysis, this research pretends to unlock the full potential of 3D-printed mortars with marble powder, paving the way for their adoption in a wide range of architectural and construction applications. This innovation promises to not only encourage the integrity of building components but also to raise their aesthetic materiality.
