TY  - JOUR
TI  - CASE SERIES ANALYSIS OF UNDERGROUND TUNNELLERS WITH CHRONIC SILICOSIS, IN SYDNEY, NSW
PY  - 2024
JO  - Internal Medicine Journal
DO  - 10.1111/imj.21_16398
UR  - https://doi.org/10.1111/imj.21_16398
AB  - Background: The re-emergence of silicosis amongst Australian workers has been recognised as a significant occupational health issue. Focus has largely been on exposure in engineered stone workers and there is little literature regarding prevalence or prognosis for other occupations in Australia. Aim and Objective: The overall aim is to describe the growing re-emergence of silicosis in tunnel workers identified through workplace medical surveillance between January 2023 and January 2024. The specific objectives include describing occupational history and risk factors as well as the clinical and radiological features in tunnellers diagnosed with silicosis. Methodology: A case series analysis was performed on consenting workers diagnosed with silicosis during annual health monitoring. Medical surveillance was undertaken on 213 workers on a major tunnelling project in Sydney NSW, over a year, using guidelines developed for workers in the engineered stone industry which recommend all high-risk workers be screened with high resolution computed tomography scans. Results: There were 13 cases of confirmed silicosis and three probable cases identified. Discussion: Whilst the majority of tunnellers with silicosis had over 10 years of cumulative dust exposure, two of the workers met the definition of accelerated silicosis. All workers had normal chest x-rays the year prior and only one had abnormal lung function, suggesting an urgent need to update the minimum legislated screening requirements. Given the major increase in infrastructure projects including the construction of tunnels in Australia in the last 20 years, there is likely to be a substantial number of exposed workers with undiagnosed silicosis, as well as a larger cohort with latent silicosis.
