AB  - Several challenges to climate change and the circular economy feed the current and growing demand for sustainable new materials. In the case of the construction industry, carbon emissions and natural resource depletion are issues of special concern. Waste marble (WM), which is currently produced during the processing of naturally available marble rock worldwide, is a potential risk to contamination of the environment. Therefore, the development of WMs for use in concrete mixes is of great interest. In this study, a WM sample was characterized using X-ray diffraction (XRD) and scanning electron microscopy (SEM). The sample was then tested as additive (with 0 %, 3 %, 7 % and 10 % of WM respect to cement content) in the preparation of concrete mixes. Results showed that WM is mainly composed of calciteand quartz. The particle sizes were typically between 10 µm and 20 µm but submicron particles were also observed. We also found some improvements in the compressive strength of the concrete mixes after 28 days of curing, particularly with both 3 % and 7 % addition of WM. However, an increase in the WM content from 0 % to 10 % did not improve the compressive strength, indicating that there is a limit to the beneficial addition of these WMs to concrete mixes. Further analyses by Fourier transform infrared spectroscopy (FTIR) suggest that small amounts of WM may contribute to the enhancement of the formation of calcium-silicate hydrate (C-S-H).
