UR  - https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.17756806
AB  - Despite the technological improvements introduced in the production of ornamental rock, sludge is inevitably generated in different extraction and transformation processes. The unsafe disposal of this sludge is one of the most significant environmental problems in the industry. However, this fine waste can satisfactorily fulfil the requirements of fine aggregate or filler (as supplementary cementitious material) for its use in cement-based mortar. There are studies on marble, limestone, granite, and basalt sludge, but there are none on one of the most appreciated stones by architects due to its black colour and mechanical properties, diabase, which is a fine-grained magmatic rock. This study marks the first systematic investigation into diabase sludge, specifically focusing on its characterization and evaluation of pozzolanic properties. Conducted on samples sourced from Villar del Rey, Spain, the research involved a comprehensive analysis of diabase sludge's chemical, mineralogical, and physical attributes. The findings suggest that the sludge, characterized by its acidic chemistry (SiO2+Al2O3+Fe2O3~70%), high equivalent Na2O content, and fine particle size, holds promise for displaying pozzolanic activity. Stereomicroscope examination revealed diverse materials within the diabase sludge, including wood fibres and slate particles. The specific gravity of the diabase sludge is higher than that of granite waste fines but not its bulk density. The comprehensive characterization of this particular industrial sludge demonstrates the need for careful analysis before utilizing diabase sludge in mortar. A deeper understanding of the product can help mitigate undesirable outcomes and facilitate innovative improvements. By separating the system's critical components, this research intends to pave the way for developing innovative mortars in the construction sector, evaluating the feasibility of valorising this waste as a component of more environment-friendly mortar to further circular economy.
