JO  - Routledge eBooks
DO  - 10.4324/9780429355660-95
UR  - https://doi.org/10.4324/9780429355660-95
AB  - We shall now proceed to describe the Formation of an Aquarium, as Mr. Gosse terms the water-tank, or receptacle, wherein aquatic animal and vegetable life may be preserved and exhibited in all its freshness and beauty. Others say Aqua vivarium, preserving in the compound term the ideas of both water and life; and this is perhaps the more proper, although the more awkward name. So much for the name , now for the thing . First as to form and size: these particulars must be regulated very much by the place which the Aquarium is intended to occupy, and the means and taste of the proprietor. That which has to be procured is simply a glass tank, with as much interior space as is compatible with the necessary exterior limitations; it may be of any form which presents flat surfaces to the eye, for although spherical and cylindrical receptacles are sometimes used, they are objectionable, on account of the distortion of the objects viewed, caused by the unequal refraction of light. A square or oblong form is perhaps the best of any, as it involves the necessity of fewer joints, and less frame-work to obstruct the view. Plate glass, back and front, set in a sufficiently stout angular frame of zinc, with slate for the bottom and ends, are recommended by Mr. Warrington, who by his experiments about five years ago, on the preservation of fish in a healthy state in a confined portion of water, seems to have originated the idea of the aquarium. The cement used to connect the materials should be white lead ground in oil, to which an equal quantity of red lead has been added. Care must be taken that the cement is perfectly dry and free from smell before the living tenants are placed in the tank, the size of which should be, at least , fifteen inches long, and about a foot wide and deep; the greater the advance which can be made upon this size, the more interesting and various may be the experiments and observations. The cover of the tank should be a plate of glass, merely laid on to keep out the impurities of the atmosphere; it should occasionally be lifted to allow of free ventilation. With regard to the situation in which the aquarium should be placed, we need only observe that plenty of light is absolutely requisite, hence a sunny aspect should be chosen; it may be fitted into the window of a sitting-room, and so made subservient to ornamental purposes; or occupy a stand in a hall or conservatory. Due caution, however, must be observed in regulating the temperature of the water; for if suffered to become tepid, it will destroy many of the animals: therefore it is best during the hot summer weather, to interpose a shade of some kind, between the direct rays of the mid-day sun and the glass prison of our marine pets; this shade may be oiled paper, muslin, ground glass, or any substance which will not exclude the light – that great vital necessity of all animal and vegetable life. This brings us to speak of the Tenants of the Aquarium, which must belong to the abo
