TI  - O-084 AUTOIMMUNE MANIFESTATIONS OF OCCUPATIONAL SILICA EXPOSURE IN THE STONE BENCHTOP INDUSTRY, VICTORIA, AUSTRALIA
AU  - Stella May Gwini
AU  - Ryan Francis Hoy
AU  - Karen E Walker-Bone
PY  - 2024
JO  - Occupational Medicine
DO  - 10.1093/occmed/kqae023.0605
UR  - https://doi.org/10.1093/occmed/kqae023.0605
AB  - Abstract Introduction Artificial stone typically contains extremely high concentration of crystalline silica (90%). The Stone Benchtop Industry (SBI) in Australia started using artificial stone in the early 2000s but unfortunately practices in the sector including dry-cutting, polishing and inadequate ventilation and respirator use has exposed workers in this industry to a very high risk of silicosis. Silica exposure has also been associated with a range of other clinical complications including systemic autoimmune diseases such as systemic sclerosis, SLE (Lupus), rheumatoid arthritis and Sjogren’s syndrome. Therefore, we investigated the prevalence of a diagnosis of a systemic autoimmune condition and of biomarkers of autoimmune disease including: anti-nuclear antibodies (ANAs); rheumatoid factor (RF) and extractable nuclear antigens (ENAs) in this group of SBI workers. Methods From 2019, workers in the SBI in Victoria were offered free health screening for silica-associated conditions. Workers participated in a protocolised program coordinated by an occupational health provider, under the supervision of an occupational physician. The assessment included systematic collection of information about symptoms including joint pains, dry eyes/mouth, Raynaud’s and skin conditions, and blood was taken for assessment of erythrocyte sedimentation rate (ESR), ANAs, ENAs and RF. Results Blood-test results from 469 workers were analysed (n=453 males, mean age 41 years and n=175 with a final diagnosis of silicosis). Five workers had received a diagnosis of an autoimmune condition. RF>14 IU/mL was found in 8.5% and 3.7% of workers with and without silicosis, respectively (p=0.062). ANA was detected in 103 workers (n=52/137, 38.0% silicosis cases and n=51/236, 21.6% without silicosis, p=0.001) and was present in titres usually regarded as clinically significant in 54 workers. ENA was positive in 9.8% (with no significant differences between diagnosis groups). Discussion and conclusion In this young, predominantly male cohort of SBI workers, considerably increased rates of detectable ANAs, ENAs and RF indicate that silica exposure has triggered autoimmune activation. Whilst few workers were identified to have manifested clinical autoimmune disease currently, there is potential for a high rate of incident disease in future and longitudinal follow-up is required.
