AB  - Abstract Cement and River sand are major ingredients used in the concrete industry. During the production of cement, large energy, as well as raw material consumption and CO2 emission, is a very big concern. River sand, extracted from riverbeds and required in vast quantities, is utilized as fine aggregate in concrete. River sand is a natural material and most rivers are over-exploited to fulfil the fine aggregate requirement, creating environmental problems. Also due to the scarcity of river sand, its transportation cost is high, affecting the construction project’s overall cost. Many states have put restrictions on the excavation of sand from river beds due to environmental concerns. The present study involves dimensional stone slurry (DSS) as a supplementary cementitious material (SCM), and local crushed sand as a fine aggregate. Slurry is produced during the cutting and finishing process of dimensional stone. This waste creates many problems such as respiratory diseases for the nearby residents, workers on mine, and other nearby habitats. Mining and processing stones waste which is not useful in its condition so it can be used for the construction sector by crushing it in small particles of fine aggregate called crushed stone sand. The experimental programme included generating concrete mixes with a water/binder ratio of 0.40 employing DSS as SCM at 0%, 5%, 10%, 15%, 20%, and 25% by weight of cement, with Crushed Sand confirming to zone 2 as fine aggregate. Concrete properties were assessed in both its fresh and hardened states. strength and durability attributes of hardened concrete were assessed. Compressive, flexural, and split tensile strength tests were carried out. The mechanical strength of concrete was increased by 5% when DSS was used. For durability assessment of concrete several tests such as abrasion resistance, DIN permeability, water absorption, and ultrasonic pulse velocity were conducted on concrete. Results of DSS up to 10% added in concrete was better in durability when compared with control concrete. Thus, the present Study suggests the utilization of DSS (5-10%) with crushed Sand, not only improves concrete quality but also provides cost-effective material and helps in sustainable development.
