AU  - Azadeh Hojat
PY  - 2023
JO  - EAGE Asia Pacific Meeting on Near Surface Geoscience & Engineering
DO  - 10.3997/2214-4609.202378067
UR  - https://doi.org/10.3997/2214-4609.202378067
AB  - Summary Although extraction of building and ornamental stones has a long history, the quarry people mostly rely on their experience for the quality of the extracted blocks. In the modern and challenging international market, having products without defects is crucial to survive in the market. Therefore, there has been an increasing interest to propose efficient methods capable of providing the necessary information to optimize different stages of stone production. In recent years, ground penetrating radar (GPR) method has proved its efficiency as a rapid and nondestructive technique (NDT) to detect fractures, discontinuities, and voids in marble quarries. Regarding the desired resolution, proper GPR antennas can be used to provide valuable information from detecting large-scale discontinuities before extraction to monitoring the defects of the extracted blocks and characterizing small blocks before slab cutting. This paper presents a review of the last decade of GPR contribution to the marble quarrying industry at different scales. A variety of examples on GPR surveys in different marble quarries will be demonstrated and commented.
