AB  - The production of plants in controlled environments such as nurseries, orchards, and terraces of urban agricultural production requires the use of natural or synthetic substrates that have properties of humidity, aeration, nutrient content, etc., which allow adequate development and growth of the plants.In this sense, materials such as compost, peat, and wood waste, among others, have been identified as economical, sustainable, environmentally friendly, and highly accessible sources of the raw material necessary to generate substrates for plant growth.In particular, granite powder, GP, is a residue with physicochemical properties of alkalinity and nutrient contents, e.g., Fe, Ca, Mg, and K, which can be released into the environment and promote plant production.Therefore, its use in mixtures with other materials has been proposed to modify its properties and allow its use as a substrate for plant growth.Thus, this work aimed to study the composition effect of substrates prepared from biodegradable geomimetic composites and GP waste on the leaching of potassium, phosphorus, ammonium, and nitrate nutrients.For this, polymeric composites based on organo-clays of sodium bentonite modified with 3-aminopropyltriethoxysilane and polyesters of citric acid and glycerol were synthesized, which were used together with GP to prepare the study substrates.The results showed that the prepared substrates' composition regulates the leaching of the evaluated nutrients, with the content effect of geomimetic composites being less in the retention processes for NO3 -and NH4 + ions compared to phosphorus and K + .
