AB  - Products are responsible to fabricate using few natural resources and regenerating less waste in manufacturing according to eco-efficient concept reported by the World Business Council for Sustainable Development (WBCSD) in 1991. As a consequence, reutilization of wastes from other production chains has been substantially increasing in brick production to contribute a more sustainable construction product. This study investigates the brick samples made the mixtures of recycled granite particle wastes and potential replacement of hemp shivs (10%, 20% and 30%), together with a few amounts of cement (around 11% by mass) by using clean energy production technique. Two brick specimens of 100mmx100mmx60mm and 200mmx100mmx60mm were prepared for all testing methods. Primary engineering strength of compressive, flexural and breaking as well as water absorption for durability of the brick have examined in the laboratory per American Society for Testing and Materials (ASTM) method C67 and C140 respectively. Being typical prediction for performance of the brick, compressive strength was specified 2.5 MPa, 3.5 MPa and 4.14 MPa for sundried brick, building brick and nonloadbearing concrete masonry units (ASTM C129-06) respectively. Furthermore, specified water absorption ranged from 160 kg/m3 to 288 kg/m3 for all concrete masonry units was typically used to demonstrate durability performance of the product. Four sorts of sustainable bricks produced with oven-dry density around 1800 kg/m3 . Among them, 100% granite waste aggregate (Reference-G) brick and 30% replacement of hemp shivs in Reference-G (GH-30) brick observed the highest performance of compressive strength (4.718MPa) and water absorption (205.7kg/m3) respectively while supporting sustainable developement.
