TI  - ASSOCIATION OF RESPIRABLE CRYSTALLINE SILICA EXPOSURE WITH AUTOANTIBODIES DETECTION IN ARTIFICIAL STONE BENCHTOP WORKERS
PY  - 2022
JO  - Internal Medicine Journal
DO  - 10.1111/imj.21_15766
UR  - https://doi.org/10.1111/imj.21_15766
AB  - Background: Recent cases of silica-related diseases have been reported in Australian artificial stone benchtop workers. This has been the result of exposure to respirable crystalline silica (RCS) through the processing of artificial stone and poor workplace practices in this industry. Silicosis has associations with autoimmune disease, however currently in the literature there only exists case reports indicating any association of autoantibodies in workers with RCS exposure. Objectives: This study aimed to determine if there was an association between RCS exposure and the detection of autoantibodies in an Australian artificial stone benchtop worker cohort. Methods: An analytical cross-sectional study was performed on a study population recruited from the Victorian Stonemason Health Assessment and Research Programme disease research registry. This registry included silica-related occupational exposure history and autoimmune panel results including rheumatoid factor (RF), antinuclear antibody (ANA) and extractable nuclear antigen (ENA) subtypes. Results: A dose response relationship was observed between proportion of work involving dry processing and workers with detectable ANA autoantibodies (OR 4.15, 95% CI 1.38-12.55, p=0.039). Job Duration (OR 1.05, 95% CI 1.00-1.10, p=0.039), smoking (OR 2.12, 95% CI 1.03-4.38, p=0.042), and silicosis diagnosis (OR 2.11, 95% CI 1.09-4.05, p=0.026) also showed positive association with workers with detectable ANA autoantibodies. A dose response relationship between proportion of work involving dry processing and workers with detectable ENA autoantibodies with borderline significance (OR 7.30, 95% CI 0.95-56.02, p=0.056). Conclusions: This is the first study documenting an association between intensity of RCS exposure and detection of autoantibodies in an artificial stone benchtop worker cohort.
