AB  - Background: Recent cases of silica-related diseases have been reported in Australian artificial stone benchtop workers. This has been the result of exposure to respirable crystalline silica (RCS) through the processing of artificial stone and poor workplace practices in this industry. Silicosis has associations with autoimmune disease, however in the literature there only exists reports of individual cases of workers diagnosed with silicosis with detectable autoantibodies. Currently, there have not been any studies that have investigated the association between RCS exposure and the presence of autoantibodies in a worker population. Objectives: This study aimed to determine if there was an association between RCS exposure and the detection of autoantibodies in an Australian artificial stone benchtop worker cohort. Methods: An analytical cross-sectional study was performed on a study population recruited from the Victorian Stonemason Health Assessment and Research Programme registry. This registry includes occupational exposure history and autoimmune panel results including rheumatoid factor (RF), antinuclear antibody (ANA) and extractable nuclear antigen (ENA) subtypes. Results: A dose-response relationship was observed between the proportion of work involving dry processing and workers with detectable ANA autoantibodies (OR 4.15, 95% CI 1.38-12.55, p=0.039). Job Duration (OR 1.05, 95% CI 1.00-1.10, p=0.039) and silicosis diagnosis (OR 2.11, 95% CI 1.09-4.05, p=0.026) also showed a positive association with workers with detectable ANA autoantibodies. Conclusions: This appears to be the first study documenting a positive association between intensity of RCS exposure and detection of autoantibodies in an artificial stone benchtop worker cohort.
