TI  - Becoming disenthralled with our conventional understanding of occupational lung disease
AU  - Paul David Blanc
PY  - 2022
JO  - Respirology
DO  - 10.1111/resp.14273
UR  - https://doi.org/10.1111/resp.14273
AB  - The story of occupational respiratory disease has been a long one, but this history has not followed a linear, progressive march forward in time. Novel occupational lung conditions emerge with changing technologies, such as interstitial lung disease caused by indium tin oxide used in flat screen displays or constrictive bronchiolitis obliterans due to the introduction of diacetyl artificial butter flavouring into the commercial food industry.1, 2 But far more commonly, old-fashioned exposures that have been with us for centuries, not new-fangled ones, provide a consistent warp to the historical weave of work-related lung disease. Way back when, Victorian-era practitioners concerned themselves with dusty trades.3 In the second decade of the 21st century, however, we have been brought up short by the reappearance of the very same defining construct. While our profession may be waking up to the dangers of such jobs, for those who have been carrying out this work, day after day, year after year, the risk to health caused by dusty workplace conditions is nothing new. Despite our sputtering, new-found recognition of the enduring hazards of dust inhalation, we falsely reassure ourselves that at least occupational respiratory medicine has a firm grasp on the diseases such dusts cause: a tight-knit family of conditions that in the 1860's Zenker named pneumoconiosis (originally pneumonokoniosis).4 Ever since then, we have taken as a given that pneumoconiosis demarcates the principal pathology linked to dusty trades, even if we sometimes debate questions around its edges, such as ‘How much of coal dust lung disease is coal worker's pneumoconiosis as opposed to silicosis?’ or ‘What truly constitutes mixed-dust pneumoconiosis?’5, 6 As we apply state-of-the-art biomedical analytic approaches to better understand the adverse health effects of the dusty trades, however, it has become increasingly clear that the ill effects of dust inhalation go well beyond the familiar borders of pneumoconiosis. We thought we knew silica best.7, 8 But for silica in particular, the awful and completely preventable epidemic of disease among artificial stone benchtop fabricators has shown us that classic silicosis, even in an accelerated form, is but one of the manifestations of this work. The important role of silica exposure in pulmonary alveolar proteinosis long was obscured by the label ‘acute silicosis’.9 Artificial stone-caused lung disease has re-taught us that this pathology, quite distinct from silicosis, is an important source of morbidity in this as in other silica-intensive dusty trades.10 So too, artificial stone fabrication has given us renewed appreciation for the range of systemic autoimmune diseases that such workers are prone to.11 In parallel with clinical observations, population-based epidemiologic investigations have provided important insights into the links between workplace exposure to vapours, gas, dusts and fume with other respiratory conditions previousl
