AB  - The marble industry produces a lot of marble waste which is an environmental burden. It is necessary to recycle this waste in the concrete industry. Cement manufacturing is a risk to the environment due to the emission of Co2. The use of marble waste as cement substitution presents environmentally friendly concrete. Five different concrete mixes were prepared. Cement was replaced with marble waste (MW) at the ratios of 0, 5%, 10%, 15%, and 20% by weight. The constant water to cement ratio was 0.5. The compressive strength, the flexural strength, and the splitting tensile strength of concrete made with waste marble powder were recorded at the curing ages of 7, 28, and 56 days. The rate of water absorption and density were evaluated. The slump test was carried out on the fresh concrete to measure its workability. The results were analysed and revealed that mechanical properties of concrete are improving by using MW as a partial replacement of cement. A replacement ratio of 10% by weight of cement withmarble waste is an ideal ratio to enhance the mechanical properties of concrete and after this replacement ratio, the mechanical properties of concrete decreased. The workability and the rate of water absorption decreased with the increase in MW replacement ratio. The values of density increased with the increase in MW content compared to the control mix.
