AB  - Reviewed by: Magic in Stone: The Sylacauga Marble Story by Ruth Beaumont Cook Bert Frandsen Magic in Stone: The Sylacauga Marble Story. By Ruth Beaumont Cook. Montgomery, AL: NewSouth Books and Sylacauga, AL: B.B. Comer Memorial Public Library Foundation, 2019. 238 pp. $28.95. ISBN 978-1-5883-8404-1. Iron, coal, rivers, and biodiversity rank among Alabama’s well-known natural resources. Less familiar are the marble outcroppings in Talladega County that inspired Sylacauga’s nickname, the “Marble City.” This well-documented and richly illustrated book tells the story of Sylacauga’s marble industry from its founding in the early nineteenth century to the first decades of the twenty-first century. Over the years, production transformed from making cemetery headstones and monuments for local markets into a high-tech mineral-processing business that refines marble into calcium carbonate—a material used for making everything from toothpaste to Walmart bags. Ruth Beaumont Cook claims that the “high grade and very expensive marble lies at the heart of Sylacauga’s proud [End Page 188] heritage” (6). The author focuses specifically on the history of Sylacauga’s marble industry, the people involved, and the community they created. This is a grand story that covers a time span of centuries and reaches beyond Sylacauga to the uses of its marble throughout the United States and Europe. It is such a grand story that a reader could be forgiven for not realizing that there is more to the story of Sylacauga than marble, for these businesses employed only a few hundred people. The book neglects other important commercial activities that contributed to Sylacauga’s growth such as the Avondale textile mills. It sparsely considers the larger political and social context in Alabama, though it provides some details on slave labor during the nineteenth century and the segregated workforce during the twentieth century. What is explored in considerable depth is a fascinating story about the commercial exploitation of Sylacauga’s marble, and how the industry successfully adapted to changing economic and technological conditions over the centuries. The vein of crystalline white marble in Talladega County extends for thirty-five miles reaching its widest breadth, a mile and a half, near Sylacauga. Although the city’s name derived from Native Americans who made some use of the marble, large-scale commercial exploitation awaited its discovery by Edward Gannt, a Tennessee surgeon who accompanied Andrew Jackson’s army during the Creek War. Gannt recognized the high quality and commercial value of the marble, as did subsequent entrepreneurs, who compared it to fine Italian marble. He returned to the area during the land rush after Creek removal and founded the first and most famous of the early quarries, still known as Gannt’s Quarry. The Herd brothers, Scottish immigrants, began another quarry in the area about the same time. Although additional quarries were also established, this
