AB  - 242 Chapter 4 , “Matter(s) Hidden in Plain Sight,” speaks to the matter/form dialectic that characterized the greater part of the nineteenth century and that forecast the intellectual demise of the former to the benefit of the latter. The chapter investigates the story of matter’s so-called retreat at the hands of artists, architects, and philosophers, alike. Far from hyperbole, materiality was found in a state of ruin during the eighteenth century. Artistic and architectural imaginations were fascinated with the representation of decay, devastation, and the destruction of matter. Architecturally this was manifest in a growing disinterest for all-stone buildings, an increasing desire to build with ever-larger structural spans, and the hyper-articulation of ornamental surfaces. Intellectual anguish over the changing nature of architectural materials was pervasive during the nineteenth century, and no writer on art and architecture more emphatically communicated this concern than the Victorian John Ruskin. He had good reason to be concerned: everywhere one looked in England and France the traditional definition of stone architecture was challenged by the introduction of new material technologies. And yet, in overstating the dematerialization of nineteenth-century architecture we lose sight of alternate responses to the seeming dissolution of matter. The texts of Eugène Emmanuel Viollet-le-Duc and Gottfried Semper, for example, offer a form of resistance in their mastery of ‘composite thinking’: the former in his invention of the structural hybrid, the latter in his celebration of woven walls. Viollet-le-Duc engaged rhetorically with the structural principles of iron, a new material that challenged the tectonic behavior of most buildings and which, in combination with stone masonry, gave rise to a new theory of detailing in ‘structural composites.’ Semper, on the other hand, manifested his combinatorial imagination by valorizing all manner of building walls and enclosures which communicated the impulse to bind, knot, and weave. Whether built of wood, brick, stone, or painted marble, and whether the result of construction or the projection of imaginary beasts, Semper’s masonry bonding patterns, painted encaustic marbles, and wickerwork walls were never exclusively representational or constructional, they were both.
