AB  - Phylogeny attributes to the evolutionary history of organisms. This chapter was aimed to isolate, characterize and identify microbial flora of the marble waste-polluted ecosystem via biochemical tests and PCR-based sequencing techniques. Malt extract and nutrient agar media were used for fungal and bacterial growth and colonies isolation. Molecular analysis was done by amplifying the Internal Transcribed Spacer (ITS) region by means of ITS4-R and ITS1-F primers for fungal strains. Phylogenetic tree was made for each fungal species. The preliminary results identified a total of 26 microbes, including 6 fungal and 20 bacterial strains. Molecular identification and phylogeny of fungi resulted Aspergillus brasilensi , Aspergillus sydowii , Aureobasidium leucospermi , Fusarium petroliphilum , Curvularia aeria, and Alternaria alternata species from marble wastewater-polluted ecosystem. Anatomically, these strains range from cylindrical to round, hyaline in lactophenol blue, smooth to ornamented surface with sharp scale, and fusoid to ellipsoid in shape. Majority of the bacterial strains were Gram-negative and rod- and spiral-shaped. Biochemically, most of the cultures were positive to Methyl Red test, Voges–Proskauer test, Catalase, Simmons citrate, and indole and negative to oxidase. These microbial strains will provide a framework for future investigation with special reverences to phytoremediation of marble waste-polluted ecosystems.
