AB  - Marble waste is the principal solid waste, generated during the processing of marbles, in stone processing industries. Managing the solid waste, creates a challenge for the society. In the present study, the composites are fabricated with different proportion (0, 5, 10 and 15 wt. %) of waste marble particle (WMP), along with epoxy and glass fibre by simple polymer casting method. Erosion characterisation of the composite samples have been conducted by following ASTM G76 standard. The experiment was statistically designed as per the Taguchi&s;s L 16 model. Incorporation of WMP had shown an improvement in erosion resistance of the composites. A theoretical model has been developed to predict the erosion wear rate (EWR) of the composites. Subsequent to this, a neural network model is also developed to predict the erosion wear properties of glass-epoxy-marble (GEM) composites based on their experimental results obtained from the erosion test. The input data were trained in such a manner that neural network model can precisely predict erosion wear properties of these composites. Moreover, the experimental results are compared with the predicted data to validate the neural network model. It is inferred, from the validation that the developed model can be utilised to predict the erosion wear rate of composites up to an accuracy of 95%.
