AB  - This article discusses the case of the Ubirajara fossil based on the legislation in force in Brazil. Consequently, recent publications that report the normative structure of the country and what procedures have been performed in similar cases are analyzed. The results suggest that while the pre-published description of the fossil incorrectly reported that the fossil legally left Brazil as mineral heritage, later information indicated that the material may have been exported legally as an ornamental rock, or else that the fossil might have been illegally exported. Another point considered is that following the description of this new species, the specimen’s legal framework changed; therefore, it must be returned to Brazil because it is a holotype, which has a specific legal status. The present evaluation indicates the need to update Brazilian legal regulations to establish criteria and procedures for the management of fossils and reinforces the need to create an international legal framework that addresses the elements of geodiversity.
