UR  - https://doi.org/10.1201/9781003148760-21
AB  - Currently, one of the biggest disadvantages of marble and granite processing (MGR) is the waste it generates. The dust generated by this process requires proper disposal. This study aims to use MGR by-products in civil construction materials, thus preserving the environment. The following characterization tests were applied to the composition of the MGR powder: differential thermal analysis (DTA), X-ray diffraction (XRD), infrared Fourier transform (FTIR), laser particle size distribution analysis, as well as examination of fine particle and apparent mass. Then, the samples were prepared with the following percentages of MGR dust: 0%, 10%, 15% and 20%. Tests of water absorption, axial compression and modulus of elasticity were performed. The results obtained for the samples demonstrated that the water absorption was greater with the sample of 20%. The compression and elasticity tests were evaluated by ANOVA to validate the difference between the means. The compressive strength decreased with the addition of MGR powder, and the elasticity did not show significant differences for the mixtures tested from 7 to 35 days of curing time, considering the standard deviations of the samples. Thus, this work demonstrates that MGR is capable of replacing natural raw material in the manufacture of cementitious composite for civil construction.
