AB  - The Romans extracted the Rhenish Tuffs by underground mining with mechanical tools. The Rhenish Tuffs are marketed by trade names like Ettringen Tuff, Romans Tuff or Weibern Tuff. Tuffs from other deposits are no longer extracted. Many historic examples of application can be still seen in the previously mentioned cities, which have a Roman background. Currently, a huge number of profane, ecclesiastical and public buildings in Germany and the neighboring countries show façades, masonry, decoration elements and sculptures of Rhenish Tuffs. Rhenish Tuff is one of the German ornamental stones that is used in the whole country as well as in the neighboring countries. The Rhenish Tuffs normally have a good resistance against weathering and freezing. This is amazing in respect to the relatively high water absorption and porosity. A typical weathering phenomenon is the loss of weak components, forming holes.
