TY  - JOUR
TI  - The Effect of Fly Ash on Engineering Behavior of Expansive Soil Stabilized by Kota Stone Slurry Powder and Marble Dust
AU  - Ajay Pratap Singh Rathor
PY  - 2021
JO  - SPAST Abstracts
UR  - https://spast.org/techrep/article/view/2885
AB  - It is common knowledge that dealing with expansive soils for construction is problematic due to their unexpected volume change behaviour. One way for improving the engineering features of such soil is soil stabilization.
There are a variety of soil stabilization techniques that can be used to change the engineering properties of soil. It is normal practise to utilize waste products as a soil stabilising agent in order to properly utilise industrial waste and save the environment and land space. Stone dust, brick dust, wooden saw dust, plastic, natural or synthetic fibres, fly ash, and other waste materials are commonly used as soil stabilising agents[1], [2].
The influence of fly ash on the engineering properties of soil stabilised by Kota stone slurry powder (KSS Powder) and Marble dust is investigated in this study. Marble dust is formed during the cutting and finishing of marble, and when used as a soil stabilizing agent, it has a positive impact[3]– [5]. Kota stone slurry powder contains lime and has high binding characteristics, making it suitable for use as a soil stabilizer[6]– [9]. Initially, the expansive soil was stabilised with Kota stone slurry powder and Marble dust, which were introduced to the soil in proportions ranging from 0 to 30% at 5% intervals (5 percent ,10 percent ,15 percent ,20 percent ,25 percent, and 30 percent). The effect of Fly Ash on both samples was investigated after stabilising the soil with these two wastes[10]– [13]. Expansive soil and KSS Powder, as well as Expansive soil and Marble Dust, were mixed with fly ash. The proportion of fly ash was introduced between 0 and 30% with an interval of 5%.
Various laboratory test was performed to analyse the engineering properties of the soil such as consistency limits, swelling index, max. dry density, optimum moisture content, unconfined compressive strength and CBR value. All the laboratory tests were performed as per Indian standards[14]– [19].
