AB  - According to the results of the census, the average population growth rate compared to 1390 has reached 24.1%. At the same time, the positive urban population growth rate has been 97.1% and the negative rural population growth rate has been negative 0.73%. Estimates by the Ministry of Housing and Urban Development show that the percentage of Iran's urban population has reached more than 73 percent, while the global average of urbanization is reported at 60 percent. Therefore, with the increasing population growth, the need to provide housing also increases; which - to reduce part of the demand, the existing buildings have been demolished and renovated, which in turn leads to an increase in construction waste on a large scale. For example, the daily production of soil and debris in Tehran is 5 times that of municipal waste and 40,000 tons of construction debris is produced daily in this city. On the other hand, our country has suitable mines for the preparation of raw materials for making concrete, but its full use and reliance on long-term goals will be dangerous and harmful. Therefore, researchers proposed a solution to this problem and the optimal use of recycled materials and construction debris called recycled concrete. The use of recycled concrete from old buildings has benefited humans in recent decades, and in 1980, for example, Minsolta's transportation department covered 16 miles of a flat track with recycled concrete, and then It is estimated that approximately $ 600,000 has been saved in this project. Studies also show that construction waste makes up 50% of the total volume of waste materials in the world and in the European Union, 500 kg of construction waste is produced per capita per year. Concrete from the demolition of highways and reinforced concrete buildings in the United States and Europe has been in the recycling industry for nearly 20 years. Recycling waste from demolition for new buildings can be a good solution to this problem. Beating and grinding materials from the demolition of reinforced concrete buildings can reduce the volume of waste by about 80%. While reducing waste is beneficial, recycling it produces products that can be sold or used in other work. Therefore, due to this work and by replacing recycled aggregates with natural aggregates, the damage to the environment is reduced. If this huge amount of waste is recycled, the amount of land expected to be disposed of will be reduced and the available natural stone resources and mines will not be used up quickly, so recycling this large amount of waste will not only help preserve natural resources, but also the crisis. Growth also solves the space needed to dispose of waste. Research on recycled concrete began 20 years ago and the results are almost the same. The results indicate that recycled aggregates have an effect on the deformation and compressive strength of recycled concrete and will increase and decrease it, respectively.
