TI  - PREVALENCE OF SILICOSIS IN A POPULATION OF NSW WORKERS SCREENED USING THE SAFEWORK SILICA HEALTH SURVEILLANCE QUESTIONNAIRE IN 2019
PY  - 2020
JO  - Internal Medicine Journal
DO  - 10.1111/imj.9_14848
UR  - https://doi.org/10.1111/imj.9_14848
AB  - Background: Accelerated silicosis is of increasing concern in Australian construction workers highly exposed to ultrafine silica dust from cutting artificial or natural stone. It is unclear if silicosis is now more prevalent due to the emerging new phenotype, which has caused a more rapid progression of symptoms, disability and death. Objectives: To obtain the current prevalence of silicosis in NSW construction workers screened through the SafeWork silica health surveillance questionnaire by an occupational health provider in 2019. Methodology: A retrospective review of SafeWork silica questionnaires was performed in NSW workers screened through a national occupational health provider in 2019. Cases were defined by ILO profusion criteria of 1/0 and above or by respiratory physician diagnosis. Identified cases were divided by the sample population to obtain the prevalence. Cases were analysed to understand their dust exposure, symptomatology and respiratory function. Results: 9 silicosis cases were detected within 368 questionnaires, of which 6 were newly detected from the screening. The prevalence within the sample population was 2.5% (95% CI: 1.1, 4.6). All cases were asymptomatic with normal spirometry. All had a significant occupational history in civil construction with high levels of dust exposure. Discussion: The prevalence of 2.5% within the study is similar to the 2% prevalence in the SABRE study. However, large-scale screening of NSW workers is in its infancy. The prevalence may rise when other high-risk roles, such as artificial stone-cutters, are included into future studies. Further diligent case-finding in a wider population will improve the estimate of the true prevalence of silicosis in NSW.
