AB  - The state of Espirito Santo is the largest Brazilian producer of ornamental rocks. This economic sector plays a very important economic role, but is responsible for significant environmental degradation and workers' health negative impacts. The marble industry represents
about 60% of the productive chain and is characterized by low technological incorporation,
precarization of work conditions and poor investments in training and improvement of health
and safety conditions. Many companies operate in the informal sector, invisible to the public
regulation. This study describes the working conditions of ornamental stone productive chain
with emphasis on the hazards for workers’ health and environment in a small city of the
northwestern region of Espirito Santo. The methodology was based on conceptual approach of
productive chains and included literature review, documentary analysis, interview and observation of productive units. Sixteen companies were studied, six of formal sector. The results
show precarious work conditions, exposure to multiple hazards to workers' health, especially
breathable dust of silica, and bad practices of risk management and waste disposal. Based on
these results, health surveillance actions were proposed and organized in order to contribute to
the development of an integrated plan for the territory, involving the Primary Health Care
team and aiming at improving living and working conditions.
