AB  - Introduction: Artificial stone dust (ASD) contains high levels of ultrafine particles (UFP<1mm) which penetrate deeply in the lung. Aim: demonstrate the direct effect of UFP in the lungs of ASD exposed workers on functional inflammatory and imaging parameters. Methods: 100 workers with ASD exposure up to 20 years were recruited from small enterprises all over the country. Pulmonary Function Test (PFT) Computerized Tomography (CT) and induced sputum (IS) were performed by conventional methods. Scans were evaluated for features indicative of silicosis in 3 zones of each lung using a system scoring [1]. UFP were quantitated by NanoSight LM20 system (Nanosight Ltd., Salisbury) using the Nanoparticle Tracking Analysis (NTA) and IL-6, IL-8 and TNF-a levels by Luminex (R&D Systems). Results: after deposition of 159 of raw ASD 50% of the floating particles were UFP. In this context we observed that IS supernatant contains high % of UFP that (-) correlated to PFT9s. (TLC r=-0.347, p=0.011) FEV1 r=-0.299, p=0.046,) DLCOsb (r=-0.425, p=0.004) CT score (r=0.378, p=0.023) and inflammatory cytokines (+) correlates with accumulation of UFP in IS IL-8 (r=0.336, p=0.024) IL-6 (r=0.294, p=0.065) and TNF alpha (r=0.409, p=0.007) Conclusions: We showed here for the first time association between nanoranged particles, decreasing PFT and CT worsening together with elevated inflammatory cytokines. This may be attributed to high dose inhalation of ASD- UFP at work. [1] Begin R, Bergeron D, Samson L, Boctor M, Cantin A (1987) CT assessment of silicosis in exposed workers AJR Am J of roentgenology 148:509-514 doi:10.2214/ajr.148.3.509
