AB  - Forced labour is a social evil. With economic development, competition, and an increase in attraction towards material goods, due to inflation, poverty, or greed for money, parents engage small children in earning a livelihood. Many times, the band of greed is tied over their eyes in such a way that they send children to work even in gambling dens, beer bars, etc., which has a bad effect on the tender minds of children. Their childhood does not remain intact. Childlike emotions and tenderness are lost. They begin to mature before time. Many times, accumulated dissatisfaction creates rebellion in them and even turns them into criminals. Due to child labour, the natural physical and mental development of children is obstructed. As a result, their working capacity declines. Not only this, their future culture is also adversely affected. They are made to do tiring work for 10 to 12 hours daily, due to which their intellectual development does not take place. Because intellectual development does not occur, they remain labourers throughout their lives, and at the same time, due to being uneducated, they further increase problems such as malnutrition and population growth. Children working in various industries have to face many types of diseases and disabilities. Children working in the matchstick and firecracker industries suffer from coughing problems and face the danger of being severely burnt, whereas children working in stone quarries, slate, or glass industries become prone to suffocation due to silicosis, dust, and heat. In the handloom industry, there is a high possibility of children being affected by fibrosis and byssinosis, and in the carpet industry, due to dust and fibres, severe lung diseases, arthritis, and strain on joints. Children working in the lock or brass industries suffer from asthma, severe headaches, and tuberculosis, and child labourers in bangle factories suffer from diseases such as pneumonia and heart attacks.
