AB  - Recent expansions of stonecutting industries have led to increased water contamination. In this study, ferric chloride (FeCl3, 6H2O), alum (Al2 (SO4)3, 16H2O), and aluminum chloride (AlCl3) are used as coagulants additives to treat wastewater of stone cutting industries. Response surface method (RSM) is used to optimize the operating variables such as coagulant dosage and pH to remove Total Suspended Solids (TSS). Additionally, one of the common RSM algorithms called central composite design is used to design laboratory analyses networks. Eleven tests are designed and conducted for each of three coagulants. Results show that the highest removal efficiency of TSS and turbidity using coagulant (FeCl3, 6H2O) at pH 10.12 and dosage of .519 (g/l) is 88% and residual turbidity equals to 19 NTU. Alum (Al2 (SO4)3, 18H2O) shows the optimum performance at pH 10.62 and dosage of 1.092 (g/l) with a rate of 87% removal of TSS and residual turbidity 18 NTU. Furthermore, coagulants (AlCl3) at pH 9.23 and dosage of 0.780 (g/l) indicates the optimum removal efficiency of 80% TSS and residual turbidity of 19 NTU. Three coagulants used in this study have shown good results in the basic pH too.
