AB  - Huge amount of solid industrial waste (8000 tons per day) is generated in different industrial zones of Karachi. The characteristics of waste depend on the quality of the raw material used and various techniques and processes employed by particular industries. Many scientists in developed countries have studied how to reuse the sludge and sludge-ash into useful products for domestic and industrial operations, such as retrieving the organic components in sludge to improve farmland, palletized aggregate for concrete (Liew, 1998; Joo-Hwa et al., 2000).Replacement of cement in concrete and the production of ceramic bricks and tiles were suggested Romualdo et al. (2005) while mixing sludge and clay to make building bricks was proposed (Alleman et al., 1990; Anderson, 2002).The severity of waste problem in Karachi can be assessed from the fact that out of the 8000 tons per day, approximately 89.9% is reusable, recyclable or compostable. It is estimated that around 300,000 people are directly or indirectly involved in recycling of solid waste in Karachi. Boulder slag of steel mills of Karachi is being used in cement factories for making cement. Marble waste powder had been utilized for making red cement, calcium hydroxide and heat resisting tiles in Peshawar. Discarded polythene bags are being used in making plastic furniture etc.Most industries discharge their waste into dry streams and rivers while some others dump in the open wilderness areas. Only few industries have their own treatment plants where the waste is properly treated. In Karachi, a group of industries has developed the common effluent treatment plant (CETP) in order to economize the process, so each industry pays a minimum amount for their waste treatment. The end product of the treatment plant (sludge) is being dumped on government recognized open dumping sites while some industries use the incineration services where waste is incinerated scientifically. After the incineration the amount of waste volume is significantly reduced in the form of ash. During this long practice, an industrialist of a developing country like Pakistan has already spent a substantial amount on waste treatment (incineration) so they are not concerned with the resulting ash. The only option they have to dump it in a dumping site because there is no scientific landfill site available in Karachi. Presently huge amount of ash is stored and waiting for a suitable method of their disposal. As the amount of ash produced by the incineration, effective reuse or safe disposal of ash becomes more important. The conventional disposal method like dumping may not be a suitable solution because the leachate from the dumping site could increase secondary pollution. After spending some money on the treatment and incineration of sludge the output must be economically viable.Considering these points in mind an attempt was made to see what type of industrial waste could be used to manufacture construction blocks. Fiber and chemical
