TY  - JOUR
TI  - RELATIVE PERFORMANCE OF LOCAL MATERIALS AND INDUSTRIAL WASTES AS LINER AND COVER
AU  - Mayuresh Patil
AU  - S. S. Quadri
PY  - 2011
UR  - https://gndec.ac.in/~igs/ldh/conf/2011/articles/Theme%20-%20L%2030.pdf
AB  - Composite clay liners and covers require large quantity of soil for there construction. Hence use of local soils and industrial process wastes will reduce the cost of these. But the properties of these materials need to be altered to suit the requirements of liner and cover materials. In the present study local soils such as Kolar and Halumannu soil and industrial process wastes such as Red mud, Granulated slag and granite polish wastes are used. Initially, the basic geotechnical properties of these materials are determined. Then these properties are altered by adding commercially available sodium bentonite in different percentages and change in the permeability of these materials is studied. Also, optimum additive quantity is determined. It is observed that, the sodium bentonite has great influence in altering the geotechnical properties of these materials and it is found that the performance of these materials is encouraging. The study shows that, suitable percentage of sodium bentonite will reduce the permeability to the required range. However the relative performance of these materials helps in choosing the material for liners and covers considering their availability, cost and applicability.
