AB  - South Australia's natural stone resources are notable for their variety and widespread use. within 150 km of Adelaide, limestone, marble, granite, sandstone, slate and siltstone are readily available. This diversity of stone resources stands in contrast to other Australian cities and many other regions around the world. The planners of colonial South Australia appreciated these potential building stone resources, as shown in the instructions appointing Johannes Menge as Geologist for the South Australian Company in 1836. This paper reviews the early history of building stone quarrying in South Australia and traces its historical origins. The period of recognition and earliest use of the various stone types is established. In addition the regulation and assistance supporting the regional stone industry in South Australia is examined together with a brief review of historic exports and imports.
