TI  - Petrological characterisation of ‘alabaster’ from the Marib province in Yemen and its use as an ornamental stone in Sabaean culture
AU  - Christian Weiss
AU  - D′arne O′Neill
AU  - Roman Koch
AU  - Iris Gerlach
PY  - 2009
JO  - Arabian archaeology and epigraphy
DO  - 10.1111/j.1600-0471.2008.00309.x
UR  - https://doi.org/10.1111/j.1600-0471.2008.00309.x
AB  - ‘Yemen alabaster’, a calcareous sinter, is a unique building and ornamental stone of the Sabaean culture (c. 1000 BC to 100 AD). This study characterises the quarries and describes the facies of their stones. Through thin sections, luminescence microscopy and geochemical analyses, ‘Yemen alabaster’ can be classified and assigned a quarry source. The stone is a calcareous sinter related to volcanism in the Marib province. It can be divided into several microfacies types different in luminescence and trace element contents. During Sabaean times it was mined in highly organised quarries including underground quarries and used extensively in the Sabaean kingdom.
