PY  - 2007
JO  - The Journal of Asian Studies
DO  - 10.1017/s0021911807001052
UR  - https://doi.org/10.1017/s0021911807001052
AB  - So many fine studies have been devoted to Tao Yuanming (also known as Tao Qian) that we can hardly imagine that significant discoveries might still be made. Yet Xiaofei Tian has accomplished the remarkable and unexpected by pursuing an unusual line of inquiry. Comparing the most commonly published versions of his poems today with wordings that circulated in the past, she finds that Tao was turned into an “eternal presence” by a predilection for textual variants that made more obvious sense to the historically conditioned viewpoint of later readers, as well as by an idealization of Tao's character that stressed the traits of simplicity and naturalness. In turn, however, much of the information about Tao in three dynastic histories and the biography composed by Xiao Tong (501–531) might have derived from his creative writings rather than from any historical evidence (see chapter 2, “Who the Master Is, No One Knows”).Although Tian points out the factual impossibilities in various constructions of Tao's biography, and the last of her six chapters (“Hard Evidence: Reading a Stone”) penetrates centuries of conflicting legends surrounding a rock on Mount Lu where the poet reputedly rested when inebriated, her interest is not the exposure of fallacious connections between events in Tao's life and the contents or occasions of his poems (a scholarship ably presented in the commentary of A. R. Davis, T'ao Yüan-ming, AD 365–427, His Works and Their Meaning, 2 vols. [New York: Cambridge University Press, 1983]). Instead, her goal is to show the instability of the texts on which our understanding of Tao is founded. Thus, her book is at once a study of manuscript culture's fluid nature and an excavation of forgotten dimensions of the poet through a study of the variant words and phrases that were not adopted in the standard readings.Forces that fixed Tao's iconic status are addressed in chapter 1, “Possession and Loss.” Whereas handwritten transmissions of works generated a large number of variants (each text containing its copyist's unique lapses or deliberate emendations), the rise of printing during the Northern Song brought about greater pressure to decide which words could be deemed the original ones used by the author. Beginning with her analysis of Su Shi's (1037–1101) assertion of confidence that he had “gotten” Tao's true meaning when Su passionately advocated the choice of the more spontaneous term “catch sight of” (jian) over “gaze at” (wang) in the line, “I gaze at South Mountain in the distance,” Tian brilliantly interweaves evidence of the importance of “possessing” (de) in Song culture. Such “possession” involved the literati's anxieties about textual authenticity and obtaining genuine editions; Su Shi's concern that in lending out an ornamental rock, he might not recover it; and, notwithstanding his advice to a friend to enjoy artworks without holding onto them, his obsessive determination to acquire a certain other miniature mountain for his co
