PY  - 2005
UR  - https://dspace.unl.edu.ec/jspui/handle/123456789/18243
AB  - Ecuador is a country with a diversity of geological systems and therefore of enormous mineral wealth. The country's geological data provide us with important elements for understanding and solving fundamental problems facing mining exploration and exploitation; Such as the genesis of known mineral deposits, the location and character of others presumed to exist, the knowledge of the mining potential to be able to define the methods applicable to prospecting, exploration and exploitation of minerals already discovered or discovered.

In this context, it is very important to briefly review the current knowledge of the geology of the country in order to face the search for mineral deposits, which, as has been established, are closely related to geological evolution by their association in space and time with Geotectonic units in which it is developed. The geology of Ecuador is dominated by the mountain ranges of the Andes that limit their main geomorphological regions, which from East to West include: the Amazon Region, the Cordillera Real, the Inter-Andean Region, the Western Cordillera and the Coast.

The wealth of our Ecuadorian soil and subsoil is sufficiently large and diversified, both in metallic and non-metallic minerals as well as ornamental rocks, presenting interesting prospects for intensive mining exploitation, allowing the implementation of significant mining projects. Regarding non-metallic minerals we have the most important ones: limestone, marble, clay, gypsum, pumice stone,
Barite, and bentonite; The exploitation of limestone and pozzolana for the cement industry is a more important item in nonmetallic mining production.

In the southern part of the country there is an important development of the ceramic and artistic industry, some of whose products supply the subregional market. In the


Subandine zone and eastern region also has large deposits of siliceous sand; Thus, we find important mines that are currently in operation to supply small factories of containers of glass, ceramics and as correctors of PH of limestone destined to the production of cement. Gypsum exploitation is concentrated in the southern part of the country, in the Province of Loja (Malacatos), where the requirements of the cement industry and part of the construction are partially supplied.

Nowadays, in the Malacatos Basin, in the sites included La Merced, La Quebrada Santo Domingo (Minera Jabonillo) and San Jose, the exploitation of the plaster deposits is carried out, the same one that is carried out in an artisan and anti-technical way; Which does not allow the extraction of the mineral in a technical-mining way. This is due to a lack of national and / or foreign investment, government support and lack of technical-environmental training for mining personnel working in the areas of exploitation.

The present research project called Election of the Exploitation System for Existing Gypsum in the mining parish Malacatos, Canton Loja; Is aimed a
