TI  - Studies of the degradation of building materials by isothermal microcalorimetry
AU  - Christer Sjostrom
PY  - 2004
JO  - Routledge eBooks
DO  - 10.4324/9780203223710-41
UR  - https://doi.org/10.4324/9780203223710-41
AB  - Abstract Isothermal microcalorimetry is concerned with the measurement of very low heat production rates (thermal powers) «1 /lW) on small samples (10-1000 mg) at isothermal conditions. The technique is well established in several scientific and technical areas, e.g. in the pharmaceutical industry. Microcalorimetry is a general research tool as almost all processes (biological, chemical and physical) produce heat. In this paper is discussed its use to measure degradation phenomena on building materials, e.g. metal corrosion, oxidation of polymers and mould growth on wood. Keywords: isothermal microcalorimetry, degradation, corrosion, biological activity, polymers, natural stone, cement/concrete, compatibility 1 Introduction Nearly all processes (biological, chemical and physical) are connected with the release of heat. Such diverse degradation phenomena as microbial metabolism, oxidation and condensation all produce heat. Techniques for the measure of heat production rate are therefore general and versatile research tools. Isothermal microcalorimetry is such a technique by which very low thermal powers (/lW) on small samples (10-1000 mg) may be measured at isothermal conditions. This paper discusses a number of different types of measurements of the degradation of building materials. Some of these have been realised at our laboratory or elsewhere, and some have not been tested yet. The aim is to show that microcalorimetry is a general and powerful research tool for building material scientists.
