TY  - JOUR
TI  - ASSESSMENT OF INDUSTRIAL WASTES AS A MINERAL FILLER IN ASPHALT CONCRETE MIXTURES
AU  - Mustafa Tuncan
AU  - Ahmet Tuncan
AU  - Altan Çetín
PY  - 2000
UR  - https://trid.trb.org/view/674059
AB  - The objective of this research was to evaluate the properties and performance of industrial waste mixed asphalt concrete specimens in the laboratory. Industrial wastes such as fly ash, marble powder, rubber powder and petroleum contaminated soil (PCS) were used as filler materials in the asphalt concrete specimens. Stone powder was used replaced by these industrial wastes as a filler material. These wastes were sieved under #200 sieve. For these purposes, one type of limestone aggregate and one penetration-graded asphalt cement (70-100) were selected according to Turkish Highway Specifications. Addition of fly ash, rubber and PCS decreased the Marshall stability values by 1%, 7% and 6% respectively with respect to stone powder mixed specimens. However, addition of marble powder increased the Marshall stability value by 10%. Addition of marble powder gave the best results according to the Marshall stability test. While addition of fly ash increased the indirect tensile strength value by 1.9%, additions of marble powder and PCS decreased its value by 0.7% and 25% respectively, compared to the control specimens. Addition of fly ash gave the best result according to this test. Addition of marble powder and PCS decreased the compressive strength. However, there was no change with the addition of fly ash. There was a similarity between the results of the unconfined compressive test and the indirect tensile test. Addition of fly ash gave the best result for this test. It was found that additions of fly ash and marble powder gave the best results among them. However, addition of rubber gave the worst result among them because of its low specific gravity compared to the others. For the covering abstract see ITRD E107185.
