AB  - This paper identifies some of the most enduring and pervasive visual images of the North East of Scotland which have inspired writers with a mixture of awe, pleasure and, in some cases, mild distaste. Attention is particularly concentrated on the message these items of landscape and landscape furniture convey and how it can change over time. In preparing the paper which was originally delivered as a backcloth to the annual conference of the University of Aberdeen's Centre for Scottish Studies (November 2, 1996),1 I have pleasure in confessing to being much influenced by the publications of the British archaeologist Richard Bradley,2 the American anthropologist Bruce Trigger,3 the American historian Simon Schama - TV series and book, 4 the latter mainly centred around woodlands and forests in Europe - and my own love for, and appreciation of the landscapes of North East Scotland, approached from the point of view of a geographer. The majority of my illustrative material will be culled from what might be termed the built environment - monumental architecture - but I will also briefly touch on the natural environment of stone, particularly mountains and how perceptions of these have changed over time, from the early travellers to the present - from viewing them as terrifying and awesome to inviting and spiritually inspiring. A gathering of word associations aroused by the conference title - The North East - Wrought in Stone, quickly emerged as follows: stark, grey, hard, cold, geometric, functional, ornamental, remembrance, barrier, territorial, symbolic, inner space (presumably 'within these walls'), impediment, safe haven (fishermen), solid, stability (the Rock applied to Peter) and totems. If nothing else, word collection drew attention to the relevance when assessing the message conveyed by buildings of interior layout as well as external appearance. To start with the living stone - as it happens, our native granite - Thomas Pennant, early traveller in rebel country, wrote somewhat dismissively of the Cairngorms in the late eighteenth century
