AB  - by Sven Hernberg, MD'Occupational cancer has been one of the main areasof epidemiologic research at the Finnish Institute ofOccupational Health for many years. Although thepopulations exposed to any specific carcinogen tendto be small in a country with a population of 4.9 mil­lion only, othercircumstances render research in can­cer epidemiology fruitful. First foremost thecountry's registers are good, especially'the FinnishCancer Register, which was established already in 1952(12) whose coverage is virtually 100 010. Most ofthe cancer studies have been conducted in close col­laboration with the Cancer Register. In addition thepopulation registers are good. Thanks to their highquality, usually more than 98 % of a cohort can betraced. Furthermore, close collaboration with skilledoccupational hygienists has greatly improved the qual­ity of the exposure data used in the epidemiologicstudies. These circumstances tend, at least partly, tocompensate for the smallsize of the study populations.In 1980, at the first US-Finnish joint symposiumheld in Cincinnati, Tola reviewed the Finnish occupa­tional cancer studies that had been conducted up tothen (I3). The present overview highlights the investiga­tions that have been published thereafter.SilicaBoth patients with manifest silicosis workers ex­posed to silica dust in quarry work have been studied.A mortality investigation was conducted among 961patients with silicosis diagnosed between [935 1977and recorded in the Finnish Register ofOccupationalDiseases (8). There was a consistent finding ofexcesslung cancer deaths both in the cohort as a whole [ob­served 80, standardized mortalit y ratio (SMR) 312,99 % confidence interval (CI) 230-414] in all sub­cohorts form ed of silicosis pati ents according to thetype of industry in which silicosis was acquired, ie,mining , stone industry, steel iron foundries, and others. Confounding by smoking seemed an unlike­ly explanation for all the excess lung cancer, othercarcinogenic exposures, such as radon daughters inmines polycyclic aromatic hydrocarbons in found ­ries, may have been responsible for at least part ofit.Pure quartz exposure occurs in quarries, however.In a still ongoing project, granite work ers have beenfollowed since 197[ (6, 7). The study group is form edof I 026 workers, hired between 1940 1971 by
